WEBVTT

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.700
Foggy Williams, who calmly boots an 18-yard pressure-packed field goal, and Rice beats Texas.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.790
From out of nowhere comes Tommy Lewis, and Magle is tipped down.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.000
The senior have one of the great season for fun.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.700
of all Southwest countries' history.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.660
Look at these guys celebrating 19-17.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.780
The Owls are going to beat Texas.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.380
No quarterback and receiver have connected for more touchdowns

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.140
in college football history.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:43.800
Music

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.960
Rising from barren land on the southern edge of the emerging city of Houston, Rice Institute

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.560
was intended to create an institution of higher education that would become the finest in

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.720
the South with a reputation for excellence that would reach not just nationally, but

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.720
internationally.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.680
It was a rare opportunity to build an elite institution from the ground up, a task its

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.270
earliest undergraduates fully understood.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.000
When Rice started in 1912, it was starting absolutely.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.170
Absolutely and completely from scratch.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.470
There were no traditions, there was nothing built, it was like a newborn baby.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:47.790
And they were very, and President Lovett in particular, was very concerned that the institution

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.780
fit into the life of the city.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.810
And also very concerned that the institution build its own set of traditions.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.000
The students were very focused on this also.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.450
conscious of the fact that they were building a culture from the ground up.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.270
While Rice Institute took shape, the nation was beginning to flex its economic muscle.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:15.590
Times were good, and an emerging middle class was looking for forms of entertainment.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.140
Unlike baseball, where the professional version held sway, the game of football was becoming

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.600
increasingly popular as played on the collegiate level.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.320
Rice president Edgar Odell Lovett viewed football as the ideal offering to connect the Houston

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.000
community with his school.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.550
One month after Rice welcomed its first freshman class to campus in 1912,

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.790
members of the freshman class played Rice's first football game at Houston's West End Park.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.730
You cannot think about the history of football at Rice

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.090
without thinking about the history of the game of football.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:54.560
And that early era here was also an era of massive changes and evolution

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.650
in the game of football itself.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:00.000
1912, for example, the first year that our

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.460
Boco coached here and first year of the institution's existence, Pop Warner invented the single

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.480
wing that year.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:13.750
There had been a whole cluster of changes in the rules of football that was made at the

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:19.110
collegiate level the winter before and the intention of this cluster of changes was to

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:25.000
bring the forward pass and integrate it into a normal offense.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
College football had survived an attempt in 1905 to ban it because of its violent nature

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:37.320
President Theodore Roosevelt interceded and a committee led by legendary coach Walter Camp created a series of rule changes

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:43.100
That allowed the game to morph from its rugby roots to a wide-open hybrid that struck a chord with the public

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:50.450
Players such as Jim Thorpe who would earn his second Walter Camp All-America honor the same year Rice opened became mythical figures

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:56.020
The game grew nationally to a stage where the first postseason bowl games were created to match

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.000
Universities to try to settle the issue of who was truly the top team

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.650
To guide Rice's entry into this expanding universe,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.510
Lovett turned to an innovative mind

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.770
who had established Southwestern University

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.190
as an early power.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.330
Philip Arbuckle came to Rice to teach English and history,

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.830
as was the norm of the day,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.520
but also to impart his innovative approach to the game

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.280
on his young Rice charges.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.380
Arbuckle believed that through spacing and deception,

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.720
his relatively inexperienced Rice squad

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.060
could find success.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.440
His kind, scholarly, even-mannered approach

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.000
to Cushing football proved to be a perfect match

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.330
for his scholarly football neophytes.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.190
- He quickly had success

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.880
with a very small group of people.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.310
The first year that we played, all we had was freshmen.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.670
Virtually every male student on campus

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.530
came out for the football team because if they didn't,

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.060
we wouldn't have enough people to play.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:53.780
So he went out and very cleverly in the first year

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.180
only played a couple of smaller colleges

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.000
and some high schools, so they were able to learn

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:06.730
his system, he was able to have initial success, which, as I said, quickly caught the interest

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:07.730
of the community.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:13.860
Just how quickly Rice's athletic initiative emerged became obvious on December 8, 1914,

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.780
when members of Rice's Committee on Outdoor Sports, along with their brethren from seven

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.270
other colleges, met at Houston's Rice Hotel and established the Southwest Intercollegiate

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.270
Athletic Conference.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:30.000
In just two short years, the fledgling school on the southern edge of Houston joined with

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.300
Texas A&M, Oklahoma, Oklahoma A&M, Arkansas, Baylor, and Arbuckle's former school, Southwestern,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.020
to form a league that would soon establish its place with the top conferences in the nation.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:48.040
Arbuckle would coach the Yowls through 1923, compiling a 52-30-8 record,

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.750
with one year off for military service during World War I in 1918.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.690
The year he returned to campus proved to be his finest,

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.750
as the Yowls finished 8-1 and finished second in the conference.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:00.000
As a developer,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.600
an identity on the field, Rice's other traditions began to take shape. School colors of navy and

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.880
gray were established at the onset. The first nickname, the Grays, did not have as long a tenure.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.480
With the Athenia owl figuring so prominently in the Rice School crest, fans soon referred to the

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.840
team in kind. Along with cheerleaders, the student body sought for a way to represent their new

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.160
nickname at sporting events. The caricature of the owl began as a six-foot, 200-pound figure made of

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.920
canvas. Students would probably

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.030
the weighty symbol at football games and other athletic events.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:40.030
In 1917, the Texas A&M basketball team kidnapped the unattended owl from the gymnasium hallway

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:43.030
and spirited the hostage back to College Station.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.030
Outraged, Rice students seethe at the theft by a hated rival.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.030
The Aggies made no secret of their deed.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.030
The owl was immediately inducted into the Corps of Cadets,

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.930
and the school president posed for a photo.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:58.870
Rice student leaders hired a private investigator who sent word back to Houston

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.000
Houston, that's Sammy.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:05.070
agreed upon code word for their symbol had been located in College Station.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:11.540
The Rice students go up there in two old Fords and a Hudson to kidnap the owl back.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:17.100
They get their hands on it, but then this crazy pell-mell through the Texas countryside

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.700
chase happens with, you know, half the Corps is coming after them, you know, there's a

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:27.490
train involved, the Corps takes over the entire town of Navasota, you know, takes over the

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:30.000
telephone lines, takes over the telegraph.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:35.920
sets up roadblocks and in the end four rice students get away with a piece of

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.250
the canvas from the owl and bring it back to campus their photograph is made

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:49.310
with everyone and this is this is you know the the the triumph over the Aggies

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:56.690
and the the foundation for you know the the Sammy that they've had ever since

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.000
while the rivalry between the schools returned

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.360
to the field of play, the reminder of this incident is never far away as the Owls mascot

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.480
remains known to one and all as Sammy. Back on the football field, Arbuckle was producing players

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:15.680
who would become immortal through their contributions both on the field and in the

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:21.940
classroom. Tiny Cobb, a member of the first team in 1912, was an outstanding multi-sport athlete

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:27.390
as well as the editor of the first yearbook. And he became the first athlete of Rice's initial

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:28.250
freshman class

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.070
to be inducted

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:30.000
into the Rice Hollow

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.910
of Fame when it began in 1970.

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.610
Shirley Brick, the star of the 1919 team,

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:38.910
which lost only once, was Rice's first All-Southwest

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.350
Conference player and first owl to play

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.150
in the fledgling National Football League.

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:47.300
And there was Eddie Dyer, a member of the early Rice

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:50.300
football teams who went on to make his mark in baseball,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.300
reaching the majors as a player and then as a manager,

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.840
leading the 1946 St. Louis Cardinals

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.080
to a World Series championship.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.000
When Rice was faced with a coaching change

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:03.670
after the 1923 season, the Committee on Outdoor Sports

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.990
yearned to make headlines with a selection

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.170
of their next coach.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:11.470
The long, exhaustive search led them to John W. Heisman.

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.810
Heisman was an innovator in his own right,

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:16.440
who had successful stints at a number of schools,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.260
including Auburn, Clemson, and Georgia Tech,

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.880
where he won a mythical national championship in 1917.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.420
Heisman's hire marked a change in philosophy

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:26.920
for the football program.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:30.000
While the soft-spoken Arbuckle was a member of the faculty,

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:34.910
Heisman was hired solely to coach football and immediately demanded changes designed

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:36.870
to enhance his football teams.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:43.690
We're also in a time period where when enrollment is growing, there's stress on the budget,

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:47.570
there's stress on the facilities, there aren't enough faculty, there aren't enough classroom

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:48.570
spaces.

00:09:48.570 --> 00:10:00.000
And this guy in the football program wants more money, better facilities, and a break

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:07.070
academically, for his players. That wasn't going to happen. The boosters in town, many

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:15.290
of whom were major, major donors to Rice, they were running out of patience and insisted

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:20.550
that a change be made. The change that they made is the same change that they always make,

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:26.990
which was to get rid of the coach, rather than actually address the structural problems

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:30.000
that are at the heart of the area.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.360
issue. It proved to be Heisman's last stomp in a Hall of Fame coaching career. After the 1927

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:41.900
season, he moved to New York City, becoming the athletic director of the Downtown Athletic Club.

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:48.550
In 1935, that club began to honor the top college football player in the country. And when Heisman

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:54.930
died in 1936, the club chose to honor him by affixing his name to their award, and the Heisman

00:10:54.930 --> 00:11:00.000
trophy entered into the public's conscience. Even with the varied level of success on the

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.210
a dedicated following of loyal supporters

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:04.750
hung on every result.

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:06.070
Ye Olde College Inn,

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.870
located across Main Street from the Rice campus,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.790
was an oasis for the Rice community,

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.090
since the city of Houston

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.250
had yet to encompass the Rice area.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.640
Ye Olde College Inn's owner, George Martin,

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:18.260
hosted victory celebrations

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.800
and consoled our fans in defeat within its cozy walls.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.580
Along the way, Rice heroes and noted visitors

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:26.040
began to carve their names

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.000
into one of the tables at the Inn.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.000
College football legends, including Heisman,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:33.720
Jim Thorpe, Pop Warner, and Bear Bryant, to name just a few,

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.520
affixed their names to the table,

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.320
which became known to all as the Coach's Table.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.940
In 1926, Martin began honoring Rice's most valuable player

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.240
with an award that now bears his name.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:48.990
It became almost immediately a tremendous honor

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.010
and drew a lot of attention to it.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.380
And people worked really hard, coveted that award.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.700
I think the reason that it's lasted so long is that

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.140
You know, at a small school, at a school that sometimes has not had success on the field

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:14.470
to really bind people together, it really does become a deeper and more personal attachment

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:19.000
that, you know, you don't just toss that away.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.920
Winners were presented as shotgun as Martin Award winners for many years.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:30.000
Today, winners receive a trophy, but all are bonded as winners of Rice's oldest athletic honor.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:32.580
tradition that began when Wash-Heavy

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.240
Underwood won the first of two

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:36.840
consecutive MVP awards in 1926

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.460
links Rice's earliest

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:40.620
standouts to their brethren today.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:42.860
By the mid-1930s,

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:44.280
Rice's leaders and supporters

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.290
sought to address issues they felt were

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.470
impeding the success of the football team.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.430
Rice was still a newcomer on the

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:52.370
college scene without an established athletic

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.410
tradition, and recruitment of athletes

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.630
was not yet a generally accepted practice

00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:58.310
across the country. Rice

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:00.000
adhered to the strictest of academic

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.080
was steadfast in not bending them to accommodate athletes.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:08.900
At the same time, there was a cry to address the glaring shortage of qualified teachers

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:12.240
and coaches to guide the swelling population of the region.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:13.300
The solution?

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.870
Create a physical education program funded solely by the Houston business community that

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:23.110
did not deviate from Rice's high academic standards, but also met the mission of the

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:25.930
school to impact its community in a positive way.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:30.000
President Lovett agreed, and within a few years of its launch in 1990,

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:34.940
29, the program would reap benefits for Rice in both regards.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:43.530
We did begin to produce better graduation rates and student athletes who went on to have solid

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:52.110
careers in Texas high schools, which later set us up for recruiting success in the state.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.060
Rice Institute had made remarkable strides in its first 20 years.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:14:00.000
The roaring 20 saw it enter the national conversation both academically

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:04.310
athletically, but the strength of that foundation would be severely tested when

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:08.170
the stock market crashed and America lapsed into what would become the Great

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:12.730
Depression. Over the next 20 years, as the country struggled to rebound and the

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:17.930
world raced full speed to an even larger global conflict, Rice proved to be a model

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:22.870
of progress, providing larger groups of students the finest education and sports

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:28.130
fans throughout the region a vital diversion from their daily challenges.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:54.430
Against the backdrop of economic depression at home and ominous political changes abroad,

00:14:54.430 --> 00:15:00.000
Rice Institute entered its third decade as a vibrant oasis where a new generation of

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.580
were honing skills that would soon be tested far greater than any could have imagined.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:10.060
On the football field, a wider array of talented players who were not dissuaded by Rice's

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:15.360
academic challenges were gathering on campus, and soon enough, the Bayou City would get

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.380
its first moment in the National Gridiron Spotlight.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:24.020
Thanks to the adoption of a physical education program, head coach Jack Maher had begun to

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:28.950
attract more and more talented players to South Main, winning eight games in 1930,

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:30.000
7 to 9.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.300
1932, and shut out Texas in consecutive seasons. However, he moved on to coach at Auburn after

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:42.140
the 1933 season, and it fell to Jimmy Kitts, who had originally come to Rice to coach basketball

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:47.240
to realize the full results of Mahar's work. Kitts earned his reputation on the basketball

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:51.980
court both as a star player at SMU and as a high school coach when he led Athens, Texas

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.310
to a pair of national titles, but he proved to be a quick study when it came to coaching

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:00.000
football. In his first year at the helm, Kitts led the Yowls to

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.460
their first Southwest Conference Championship, finishing with a 9-1-1 record overall.

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:12.220
And if not for an early 9-9 tie with LSU and an upset 7-2 loss to TCU in the second-to-last

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:16.340
game of the year, the Owls might have found themselves in contention for the Rose Bowl.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:21.960
This group of Owls were, no doubt, Rice's greatest team to not reach postseason play.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:26.640
As a team, the Owls made their first foray into the top ranks of the National Polls,

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.800
finishing fifth in the Dickinson rankings.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:30.000
individually

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:33.200
Bill Wallace made an equally impressive national splash,

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:36.080
becoming the first Al to earn All-American honors.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.720
John McCauley teamed with Wallace in the backfield,

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:43.880
and the duo jointly became Rice's first two-time All-Southwest Conference selections.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.000
Wallace's impact on the game was significant enough

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.690
that in 1978, he was inducted into the College Football Hall of Fame.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.010
In fact, a total of eight players on the 1934 team

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:58.360
would go on to be inducted into the Rice Athletic Hall of Fame,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:00.000
including future Rice Athletics

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.540
director Red Bale, who dedicated most of his professional life to his school as both the coach

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:12.200
and administrator. The 1934 Owls were truly worthy of postseason play. However, with the

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:16.620
Sugar and Orange Bowl still on the drawing board for the following season, there were no options

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:21.500
available. At various times, there had been a Dixie Classic played in Dallas, but it had shut

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:27.970
down the year before. It was in 1936 when Dallas Oilman Jay Curtis Stanford announced the creation

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.000
of a game that would become the ultimate goal

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.180
for generations of gridders throughout the region.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:37.960
The Cotton Bowl began with modest intentions and the initial contest between TCU and Marquette

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:39.170
lost money.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:43.650
But spurred on by the rapidly strengthening national prestige of the Southwest Conference,

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.130
it would quickly grow into a national treasure.

00:17:46.130 --> 00:18:00.000
It was the second playing of the Cotton Bowl and we beat Colorado 28-14 and it set off a

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:09.630
football frenzy back in Houston and sort of was led to the second step in the upward trajectory.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:16.970
Kitts' Owls follow their memorable 1934 season by winning 13 games the next two years and

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:21.020
open 1937 with a pair of shutout losses and a tie.

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:25.220
But just when it appeared the season was in jeopardy, sophomore quarterback Ernie Lane

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:30.000
sparked the Owls to go 6-1-1 over the final eight games to capture their

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.940
Second Southwest Conference title in four years, and a berth in the second Cotton Bowl.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:40.020
Rice's reward for his late season surge was the chance to face an unbeaten Colorado team,

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:44.340
led by one of college football's immortal figures, Byron Whizzer-White.

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:50.270
Lane had garnered recognition of his own as Grantland Rice dubbed him college football's greatest sophomore back.

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:57.090
In front of a crowd of 32,188 fans, that nearly double the size of the previous year's crowd

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:00.000
who watched Sammy Baud lead TCU to a win in the first cup.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:05.570
Bowl. Both Lane and White lived up to their billing. White sparked the Buffaloes to a quick

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:10.810
14-0 lead in the first quarter, the second score coming on an interception of Lane. But Lane shook

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:16.110
off the slow start and threw three touchdowns and rushed for a fourth to turn Rice's first

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:22.910
Bowl experience into a winning one, 28-14. It would be nearly 50 years before Boston College's

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:28.060
Doug Felitti would knock Lane's three TD passes off the Cotton Bowl record book. White would soon

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:30.000
and leave football behind to begin a celebration.

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:34.410
practicing law that eventually led him to a seat on the U.S. Supreme Court.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:39.170
Two years after his second conference crown, Kitts would leave Rice for Virginia Tech in

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:41.750
the aftermath of winning just one game in 1939.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:46.970
Kitts' departure was hardly the news of the day, as war had broken out in Europe, and

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:51.390
Americans who were still breaking free from the shackles of the Depression now were confronted

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.190
with knowledge they could not stand on the sidelines of this conflict for long.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:20:00.000
The Owls football program had tasted great success in the man who would follow Kim.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.760
and guide them through the challenges of the war years

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.400
and onto even greater heights

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:07.500
was wrapping up his most successful season with Clemson

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.120
with a win in the Cotton Bowl.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:11.620
It would not be the last time Jess Neely

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:15.260
brought home the Sanford Trophy as Cotton Bowl champions.

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.920
Neely had been a star player at Vanderbilt

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:19.700
before embarking on his coaching career.

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:21.980
From the first moment he stepped on campus,

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:24.360
his focus and meticulous preparation

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.710
combined with a stern dose of discipline

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.930
earned the immediate respect of his players

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.000
despite the world,

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.660
wind of events taking place throughout the world. Over 27 remarkable seasons, Neely established

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:41.920
himself as the most iconic figure in Rice football history. Four times in a 12-year stretch,

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:47.070
his ELs won at least a share of the conference title. He led the ELs to the Cotton, Orange,

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:51.880
and Sugar Bowls, capturing three bowl championships, posted 14 winning seasons,

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:58.790
and saw his ELs ranked as high as fifth in the national polls. Neely developed 11 All-Americans

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:00.000
and 43

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.560
All-Southwest Conference players,

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:04.000
including All-American and three-time

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.540
All-Conference player Joe Watson,

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.740
the most decorated interior lineman

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.780
in Rice history, and Weldon Humble,

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:11.320
an All-American and two-time

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.340
All-Conference player who went on to become

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.320
the Owls' first inductee into the

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:17.580
National Football Foundation Hall of Fame.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:19.700
Neely also sent a generation

00:21:19.700 --> 00:21:21.600
of future captains of industry into

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.560
the business world, battle-tested

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.740
and well-prepared to launch careers

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.780
that have shaped much of the business landscape

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:30.000
of the region. If you look

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:33.920
through the old files, the correspondence about the hiring,

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:36.920
they talk about Neely's ability as a coach,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.910
but even more than that, they talk about something

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:44.930
that the Rice people are also deeply concerned about,

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:48.180
and that is his character.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:53.570
They talk about his commitment to the students as students.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:57.810
They talk about his honesty, his integrity, and his good nature.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:59.800
And that seems to have been

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:03.060
a huge factor in why he was hired here.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:04.980
- He was a good man.

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:09.880
He was a good coach, I can tell you that he did well.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:14.230
Of course, Neely's pretty secure with his record.

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:18.810
He doesn't, you don't have to defend Neely on anything

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:22.540
'cause his record's laid out there for everybody to see.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:27.650
He get right down and pointed and I want you to do this.

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:30.000
That's all I want you to do is that.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:37.210
And it wasn't a screamer, but it was certainly put out that you knew he meant what he was

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.800
talking about.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:44.040
The launch of the Neely era was marked by both great success and grim reality.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:50.460
The Owls went 13-5-1 over his first two seasons, capped by a 6-0 win at SMU to end the 1941

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:51.350
campaign.

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:55.610
But any chance to savor the end to that season was cut short the following day, when the

00:22:55.610 --> 00:23:00.000
nation was shocked to be told that Japan had launched a sneak attack on U.S.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:05.400
forces at Pearl Harbor. Ready or not, America was again in the middle of a global conflict.

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:10.440
Neely and all his fellow college coaches had to work around wavering rosters and players being

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:14.640
called to military duty. While some schools opted not to field teams during the war,

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:19.650
Neely's Owls found a modest level of success against a schedule that included teams from

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:24.690
local military bases that sometimes featured former teammates and rivals. Weldon Humble

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:27.070
earned national honors at Rice in 1942,

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.000
then found himself earning All-American honors

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.500
at Southwestern Louisiana Institute, now Louisiana Lafayette, while continuing his

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:39.180
military training, and then returning to Rice after the war to earn Consensus All-American

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:45.740
honors in 1946. Guard H.J. Nichols would become Neely's first Rice All-American after opening

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:50.620
huge holes for running back George Walmsley, who became the first owl to lead the Southwest

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:55.880
Conference in rushing. Countless owls went on to serve their country in the war. When hostilities

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.480
came to an end in 1945,

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:00.000
many returned home ready to resume

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.180
their studies, as well as their football careers.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:07.470
They had football players, played two careers of college ball,

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:13.160
went in the service, played two more years, and then came back.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:14.750
They were veterans.

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:16.970
They'd played four, some of them five years.

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:22.980
I didn't understand it at the time, the complexity and the situation,

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.820
but those guys were great.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:30.000
The football, but then again, the whole team,

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:37.230
era. I mean, the whole football programs were all that way. Texas had great team. SMU had

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:43.520
great team. And that's what it was all about. Those dadgum people came back and they were

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:49.610
all over the place. Post-war America was bursting at the seams with a pent-up energy caused

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:54.610
by years of sacrifice and rationing. Americans wanted to get out, spend money, and revel in

00:24:54.610 --> 00:25:00.000
their freedom. College football was only too ready to provide the entertainment. Neely's

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:05.570
1446 Owls opened with a one-point loss to LSU, but then rolled to nine wins in the last

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:11.090
ten games to hand their coach his first Southwest Conference crown and earn a date in the Orange

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:14.010
Bowl against his home state university, Tennessee.

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:17.030
The matchup was a study in contrasts.

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:21.530
The Vols were led by legendary coach Bob Neeland, who stubbornly held on to his old school

00:25:21.530 --> 00:25:23.430
ways, including leather helmets.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:28.030
Neely's team wore new plastic helmets and brought a modern approach to the field as

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:28.310
well.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.000
The war veterans who were

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.080
were the backbone of the Owls determined to close their season with a statement, but they

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:38.910
were equally determined to enjoy the fruits of their labor with the trip to Miami.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:45.830
Those guys, all of that 42 team went to the Marines, they spent in the Pacific.

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:50.260
They were over there four years killing Japanese all over the place.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:53.440
They wanted to have some fun and they did.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:56.170
Neely backed down, he did.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:00.000
The final score of eight to nothing hardly told the story of the Owls dominance over

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:05.610
Carl Russ and Hugh Keeney combined on a 50-yard touchdown on the Al's second series, and they

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.410
added a safety on a blocked punt later in the quarter.

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:12.810
After that, Neely was content to call off the dogs and let the defense hold his home

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:17.850
State University in check to register Rice's only shutout bowl victory.

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:22.490
A happy group of Al's, with all missions in Miami accomplished, headed home with a final

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:25.240
ranking of number 10 in the nation.

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:30.000
While 1947 and 48 were winning seasons, they also were the testing ground for a group of

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:33.760
who would take the Owls to their greatest season in 1949.

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:39.320
Led by All-Americans Joe Watson, two-time All-Conference standout James Froggy Williams,

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:44.240
and Tobin Rowe, the only man to quarterback a team to both NFL and AFL titles.

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:48.260
The 1949 Owls were undefeated Southwest Conference champions

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:54.960
and recorded Rice's first ever 10-win season, a feat that would not be duplicated for 59 years.

00:26:55.400 --> 00:26:59.010
The Owls' march to the title included wins over three conference foes

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:00.000
who had won their bowl games.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:06.640
after the 1948 season, as well as one of the most electrifying comebacks in rice gridiron annals.

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:14.630
Down 15-0 to Texas, the Owls storm back to score 17 unanswered points, culminating with a game-winning

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:22.300
field goal by Froggy Williams. When it was so bad at the half, Neely changed and put in a guy named

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:30.000
Vernon Glass. In the middle of the third quarter, he put him in and he got two touchdowns in a field goal. So what

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.820
can I say? He was great.

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:32.920
And I'm a hero.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:35.190
Everybody

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.290
thinks that's where

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:39.110
I got all the credit.

00:27:39.890 --> 00:27:41.630
And that wasn't me.

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.670
The guy that won the game was Vernon Glass.

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:45.700
While the drama of the Texas win

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:47.580
resonates with Rice fans this day,

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:49.820
Williams and his teammates regarded the win

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:51.800
one week earlier over an SMU

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:53.880
team that had won the previous two Southwest

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:55.880
Conference titles as the game that

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:56.880
defined their season.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:00.000
My best game was the SMU

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:00.320
game.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:02.580
The SMU had won it two years in a row.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:09.660
They were favored to win it, and we got up and we said, that was the team we had to beat.

00:28:10.540 --> 00:28:15.390
Facing the legendary Doak Walker, who had won the Heisman Trophy the year before, the

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:21.330
Owls fell behind 14-0 in Dallas, only to storm back and take a 41-27 shootout.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:26.750
Rice's 10th win came in the Cotton Bowl, where Billy Burkhalter scored both through the air

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:30.000
and on the ground, and Williams grabbed the final TD.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.860
reception of his illustrious career to provide the offense as the Owls blasted through North

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:42.060
Carolina 27-13 and finished fifth in both the AP and UPI polls. Neely's unprecedented

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:47.080
success had generated intense interest in Houston and that interest now overwhelmed

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:52.310
Rice's current football home. The original Rice Stadium had grown from a simple structure

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:56.280
surrounding the Rice Track on the corner of University and Maine, but there were only

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:59.510
so many places to jam in additional temporary seats.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.500
burgeoning group of rice businessmen, the only solution was a new stadium. One that was a symbolic

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:10.860
statement of the success that Neely's teams were achieving on the field. Days after winning the

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:16.280
Cotton Bowl, a bond drive was launched to reach that goal. And in only a few weeks, all of the

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:22.460
$100 bonds were sold and shovels were in the ground. Led by George R. Brown, whose company

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:27.730
Brown and Root took on the challenge, community leaders committed to a staggering project, both

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:30.000
scope with 70,000 seats

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:33.220
and in time frame to be ready for the 1950 season.

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:38.440
But showcasing the same legendary determination that was Texas' calling card throughout the world,

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:43.920
the project was completed on time as Rice welcomed Orange Bowl champion Santa Clara

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:48.360
to its new show play stadium on September 30, 1950.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:52.880
An architectural marvel, Rice Stadium was built for watching football.

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:57.870
Its sight lines remain the standard against which other stadiums are measured to this day.

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:00.000
We didn't get to play in that stadium.

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:00.930
We built it.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:04.920
We tell everybody we built it, and it was really true.

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:07.880
We were number five in the nation, which is pretty good.

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:11.030
They put out those intent to buy bombs.

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.560
They sent them out, and it was oversubscribed.

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:15.660
It stunned them.

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:18.250
I don't think they ever started out

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:21.230
thinking that would be sitting there,

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:24.940
because they didn't think they'd get that much money.

00:30:24.940 --> 00:30:28.400
But the money just flowed in.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:30.000
It was so much.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:36.080
excitement in the community about this that they actually set up bleachers on

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:41.780
the site but behind a chain-link fence so that the people who lived in West U on

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:46.910
their way home from work would stop and park their car climb up in the bleachers

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.170
and watch the construction crews working because they were working almost

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:55.890
completely around the clock in order to get in order to get it built in its

00:30:55.890 --> 00:31:00.000
first two seasons Rice Stadium saw the owls when seven of eleven home

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.800
contests and produce an All-American of its own and high-flying wide receiver

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.980
Billy Houghton, who became the first of a string of rice receivers who were

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:13.260
selected to carry on the tradition Froggy Williams had established by having

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:18.580
coach Red Bale switch his uniform number from 81 to 84 for his senior year.

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:23.420
Houghton went on to further establish number 84 by earning All-American honors

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:24.040
of his own.

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:30.000
Red is the one responsible for that, and he told me about it one day.

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:34.560
When he first started, he said he recruited two or three people.

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:40.990
I don't remember who they were, Houghton and Buddy, but there are about two or three other

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:43.410
all-conference guys that had 84.

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:45.740
Red recruited them and gave them that number.

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:51.900
The opening of Rice Stadium represented the dawn of a new day for Rice football.

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:55.580
At the same time, college football was entering a new day as well.

00:31:56.020 --> 00:32:00.000
Freshmen who had been eligible for varsity play in the years immediately after

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:03.450
were now not allowed to play varsity football.

00:32:03.450 --> 00:32:05.530
Rice fans would soon learn to check the schedule

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:08.130
for the Rice Owlitz because an exciting new crop

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.330
of players who would soon make their own mark

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:13.330
on the college landscape were taking their first steps

00:32:13.330 --> 00:32:14.960
into college football.

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:18.200
In the last 20 years, Rice had gone from never playing

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:21.910
in a ball to winning three and from being an unknown

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:23.790
to being a team comfortably placed

00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:25.990
in the highest levels of the rankings.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:30.000
It had sent its sons off to war and welcomed them home.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.000
America was about to blast through a decade of change, one that would see technology advance

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:39.740
at frightening speed and society itself following just behind.

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:44.280
Jess Neely would be there to guide his ever-changing list of young men through the dazzling change

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:47.960
that was ahead before eventually calling it a career.

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:52.630
It would be a time no Rice fan and no player under Neely would ever forget.

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:20.070
Rice football entered the 1950s no longer playing catch up to its larger rival schools.

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:24.790
Thanks to overwhelming support, the construction of their new home stadium made Rice the envy

00:33:24.790 --> 00:33:26.660
of their Southwest Conference brethren.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:30.000
The formidable structure would prove to be the catalyst to some of the most

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.500
memorable seasons in Rice history and cement Jess Neely's reputation as one of football's

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:42.400
legendary coaches. However, it didn't start out that way. A 5-5 season in 1951 was followed by

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:49.540
a 1-5 start to the 1952 campaign. And ironically, it was on the road and not at home where the Owls

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:54.580
salvaged their season. Rice closed out the year winning its last four, including three road

00:33:54.580 --> 00:34:00.000
contests at Arkansas, Texas A&M, and Baylor to set the stage for a memorable

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.610
Their strong finish, bolstered by a formidable roster of returning talent, led by running

00:34:06.610 --> 00:34:11.350
backs Kossie Johnson and Dickie Magel, and lineman John Hudson, Kenny Paul, and Richard

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:15.170
Chapman, made the Owls the preseason pick for the Southwest Conference crown.

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:17.860
The Owls would go on to prove them right.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:22.240
Those days, Rice went to a bowl game about every fourth year.

00:34:23.330 --> 00:34:28.440
And so if you won a bowl game, then the next year's freshman class would have a lot of

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:29.600
good players on it.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:38.270
And I think things started going apart when I was quitting the bowl game, so it was extremely

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:40.930
important to the program.

00:34:40.930 --> 00:34:46.710
They won eight of ten regular season games, falling only to SMU and to Bear Bryant's Kentucky

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:47.940
Wildcats.

00:34:47.940 --> 00:34:52.020
Though they tied Texas for the conference title, the L's earned the Cotton Bowl berth

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:56.310
thanks to a 20-13 win over the Longhorns at Rice Stadium.

00:34:56.310 --> 00:35:00.000
Johnson and Hudson were named All-Americans on the field, and Chapman

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:03.200
and became the first Al to earn academic All-American honors.

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:05.640
But the Cotton Bowl meeting with Alabama

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:08.100
would provide a stage for their teammate Magel

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:10.180
to enter into college football lore

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:12.260
with one of the most memorable performances,

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:15.240
anchored by one of the most distinctive plays

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:16.720
in the history of the game.

00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:20.400
After Alabama took a 6-0 lead after one quarter,

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:24.490
Magel shredded the tie defense for a 79-yard scoring run.

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:26.830
Moments later, starting from his own five,

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:30.000
Magel was again leaving Bama defenders in the dust.

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:33.340
With his eyes focused on the remaining defenders who might challenge him,

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:37.380
as well as the on-rushing Chapman who was looking to spring him with a final block,

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:43.200
Magle was hardly concerned with any movement along the Alabama sideline as he sprinted upfield.

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:48.870
But from out of that sideline burst Tommy Lewis, who had earlier scored the Tide's first touchdown.

00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:53.380
Lewis dropped Magle in his tracks, then quickly returned to a seat on the bench.

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:58.040
The stunned referees ruled that Magle would be credited with a 95-yard TD.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:00.000
in the blink of an eye,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.840
An incredible run in its own right

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:03.980
was elevated to a moment that became

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:06.060
an iconic play in the history

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:07.300
of college football.

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:09.760
The play did the impossible, making

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:12.620
Magel's final total of 265

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:14.320
yards on just 11 carries

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:15.940
a footnote in the

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:17.080
recounting of the game.

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:19.840
But it also proved to be the springboard for Magel,

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:21.120
who became an All-American

00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:23.580
and a first-round draft pick one year later.

00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:25.920
They said that they thought I could have

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:26.420
blocked him.

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.000
If I had the bad behind,

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.900
Dickie might have caught up with him. I don't know. I was the only one in the

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:40.880
stadium who did not know what had happened. I did not see the off pitch tackle.

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:47.130
Magel and the Owls looked to earn a return birth to the Cotton Bowl in 1954,

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:53.860
but they could not overcome a 28-15 road loss at Arkansas. They finished 7-3, ending the season

00:36:53.860 --> 00:37:00.000
ranked 19th in both the AP and UPI polls. Richard Chapman was named All-Southwest Conference

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:03.460
and academic All-American for a second consecutive year.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:09.840
The 1955 season opened with promise as the media again tabbed the Owls as the conference favorite.

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:16.440
But after two wins and a tie to open the year, things went astray and they dropped the last seven games of the season.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:25.360
In 1956, Rice opened with back-to-back wins over LSU and Alabama, but dropped its last four conference games to finish four and six.

00:37:25.740 --> 00:37:30.000
While there were glimpses of talent, few could have predicted heading into

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:36.200
57 that Neely and Stav had some magic up their sleeves. Led by the double-barreled attack of

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:41.120
King Hill and Frank Ryan, who would both go on to play quarterback in the NFL, the Owls caught

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:47.140
fire down the stretch after a 3-3 start. After downing Arkansas, they welcomed Bear Bryant and

00:37:47.140 --> 00:37:52.630
his top-ranked Texas A&M Aggies to Rice Stadium. While rumors during the week circulated that

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:57.340
Bryant had been meeting with officials from Alabama about their head coaching job, the Owls

00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.000
as we're preparing on the field to spring a trap

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.820
off the field for a massive turnout that would require installing temporary bleachers.

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:11.710
The sellout crowd of better than 73,000 witnessed a classic. Hill intercepted a pair of passes

00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:16.550
and constantly pinned the Aggies down with his punting. But it was his extra point after an

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:21.690
Al's touchdown in the first half that proved to be the difference in a 7-6 win that touched

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:30.000
off a celebration for the ages on the campus on South Main. Coach Bryant was more bitter about the

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:31.940
57 Rice lost than

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:33.820
any other game that he

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.790
ever coached. I visited

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:37.750
him 10 years after he went back to Alabama.

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.530
He was staying at the Shamrock Hilton.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:41.170
I went to his suite,

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:44.000
and in the middle of our conversation,

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:45.580
he got up and walked to the

00:38:45.580 --> 00:38:47.730
window, and it was overlooking

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:49.290
the Rice campus. And he

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:51.170
said, damn it, I told that desk clerk

00:38:51.170 --> 00:38:53.250
I didn't want a window that overlooked

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:54.450
that damn stadium.

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:57.810
On the strength of his performance in this epic game,

00:38:58.210 --> 00:38:59.460
Hill earned All-America

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:00.000
honors.

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:04.090
and went on to be the overall number one pick in the NFL Draft.

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:08.290
Following their big win, the Owls did not let their guard down the final two weeks of the year,

00:39:08.610 --> 00:39:14.610
blanking TCU and Baylor by identical 20-0 scores to earn their last berth in the Cotton Bowl.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:18.810
It would be difficult to talk about the 1950s and the Jess Neely era

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:24.620
without noting the work of loyal assistant coaches who've gone on to make their place in Rice gridiron annals.

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:30.000
Red Bale, a Rice alum and a longtime Neely aide, went on to serve the department as administrator

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:35.440
Joe Davis was a fixture at Rice for 27 years and earned the nickname Bloody Joe.

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:41.370
And there was Bo Hagan, a 13-year assistant, who went on to replace Neely in 1967.

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:48.790
The 1960s are now heralded as a decade of great change, and change was certainly in the air as the decade began.

00:39:49.280 --> 00:39:57.020
After 48 years of being known as Rice Institute, the school officially became known as Rice University in 1960.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:00.000
On the gridiron, the blue and gray turned in back to the

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.860
back seven and four seasons and earned consecutive bowl appearances for the only time in school

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:10.700
history. The Owls were surprise entrants in the 1961 Sugar Bowl where they battled an Ole Miss

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:15.600
squad that was battling for a share of the national title. Led by All-America quarterback

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:20.240
Jake Gibbs, the Rebels were prohibitive favorites against the Owls who entered the game with three

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:24.960
losses. But Ole Miss quickly learned that their opponents from Houston were not interested in

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:30.000
participating in a coronation. Rice dominated play for most of the game, but could only

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.180
turn one of five trips to the red zone into points, and a late-scoring run by Giggs sealed

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:37.220
a 14-6 Ole Miss win.

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:42.740
We weren't favored, but I don't know about public sentiment, but there was no sentiment

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:46.160
like that amongst ourselves.

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:53.610
Back then, football was a very popular sport at Rice, and I had quite a few friends who

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:59.320
were academics, as we called them, and there was a great opportunity.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:06.020
Rice turned out probably a lot larger percentage of its students than Ole Miss did of its.

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:12.280
Less than a year later, Rice earned a consecutive bull berth for the first time in its history.

00:41:12.280 --> 00:41:16.380
But the berth was bittersweet since a berth in the Blue Bunnet Bowl meant playing an extra

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:20.780
game in their home stadium and staying in their campus quarters rather than traveling

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:22.160
to another city.

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:28.810
The 33-7 loss to Kansas would prove to be the Owls' last bull appearance for 45 years.

00:41:28.810 --> 00:41:30.000
Even though the following season

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:34.620
The Owls, led by All-American center Malcolm Walker, were poised to meet Oregon in the

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:38.400
1963 Sun Bowl on the heels of a 6-4 season.

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:43.720
But the team voted not to accept the bid, and it was rival SMU who trucked to El Paso

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:45.740
and upset the Ducks.

00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:50.380
While Rice Stadium has not proved to be an ideal location for the Owls to go bowling,

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:53.820
it was becoming an historical landmark for other activities.

00:41:53.820 --> 00:42:00.000
In September of 1962, President John F. Kennedy had challenged the nation to land a man on

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.220
by the end of the decade Houston's reputation in the world was now growing

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:10.500
at a breakneck pace locally Houston was no longer a one-team town where once

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:14.340
rice football had been a solitary stalwart in the collective conscience of

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:18.490
Houston the city now had multiple sporting options first there was the

00:42:18.490 --> 00:42:22.450
Houston Oilers of the fledgling AFL followed quickly by the birth of the

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:26.420
first major league baseball franchise south of the Mason-Dixon line a few

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:30.000
miles east of South Maine, the University of Houston was making a

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:33.460
rapid ascension of its own on the college football landscape.

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:38.540
Rice Stadium's decade-long dominance as Houston's signature sporting venue was about to be eclipsed

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:43.460
by a new structure rising out of the earth just a few miles further south off of Main

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:44.320
Street.

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:49.040
The Astrodome, as it would soon be called, would prove to be a promoter's dream both

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:54.200
for the building itself and for the community who sought to use its technological advances

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:59.480
as further proof of Houston's place at the center of the new technological world.

00:43:00.190 --> 00:43:05.090
You would see that almost 75% of those season ticket holders here all of a sudden start shifting

00:43:05.090 --> 00:43:08.070
and their corporations and businesses start going to the Astrodome.

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:12.720
The Oilers played here for a while, but University of Houston all of a sudden became a respected

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:16.710
university at the time as far as athletically.

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:21.970
A tremendous difference in what the city had to offer, and of course then you have the Astros

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:26.550
come on board and the Rockets, and all of a sudden there was a whole lot of competition

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:28.230
for your entertainment dollar.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.300
In the midst of all this rapid change and rise of competition, administrators at Rice

00:43:34.300 --> 00:43:40.000
faced their own economic realities in 1965 and discontinued the practice of offering

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:41.380
free tuition.

00:43:41.380 --> 00:43:45.340
This change had an immediate impact on the number of scholarships that could be offered,

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:49.260
a challenge that a younger coach might have chosen to fight publicly.

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:54.300
But not just Neely, who stoically accepted this development, as he had all the others

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:57.510
in his nearly three decades of service to the school.

00:43:57.510 --> 00:44:00.000
Because last teams were never short of individual talent.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.880
only short of a high enough quantity of talent.

00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:06.700
That talent rose up on occasion to give the Yals memorable moments,

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:11.360
such as the upset of defending national champion Texas in Austin in 1965.

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:14.720
But those moments were becoming fewer and far between,

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:19.080
and it was announced that the 1966 season would be his last.

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:25.660
Neely's final team provided a sparkling debut with a 17-15 upset win over an LSU team

00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:28.800
that had finished 1965 ranked eighth in the nation.

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:30.000
However, seven.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:34.060
straight losses followed four of them by no more than a touchdown but his

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:38.620
dedicated seniors made sure his last day on the sidelines at Rice Stadium would

00:44:38.620 --> 00:44:45.760
be memorable presenting him a 21 to 10 win over TCU the 144th of Jess Neely's

00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:54.470
career at Rice I don't know a coach who didn't respect coach Neely and you know

00:44:54.470 --> 00:45:00.000
he had been here a long time and still recall how controversial it was

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.490
when Rice decided that it was time to move on.

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:06.910
And Coach Neely, whatever age he was, late 60s,

00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:09.880
was nowhere near ready to stop coaching.

00:45:10.590 --> 00:45:14.490
The players, you know, loved him as much as you can love a coach

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:17.730
and played for him with all their hearts.

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:23.350
And I don't think anybody ever accused Jess Neely of being unfair.

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:26.900
Following a legend is never easy,

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:30.000
but Bo Hagan knew that following Jess Neely's legacy

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.960
was only part of the challenge he had in front of him.

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:37.140
While Hagan's success was modest, especially in comparison to those who followed him,

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:42.400
the Hagan era was significant in many ways in that it ushered in a significant culture change

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:47.780
when in 1968, three African-American players were included in his recruiting class.

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:52.880
Mike Tyler, Rodrigo, Roy Barnes, and Staley Vinson came to Rice

00:45:52.880 --> 00:45:55.460
for the same challenge as their fellow freshman recruits.

00:45:55.640 --> 00:45:59.260
They wanted to test themselves in one of the nation's top football conferences

00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:00.000
businesses.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.940
gaining an education at one of the nation's finest universities.

00:46:03.940 --> 00:46:08.340
But each also knew that they were also opening a door for the first time for generations

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:09.520
to come.

00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:14.340
Vincent would make history as the first African-American quarterback in the Southwest Conference.

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:19.010
And all three would go on to prosper both on and off the field.

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:20.850
At that time, it was a big challenge.

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:30.000
Rice University, a very prestigious institution with a history of being excellent and also

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.680
uh... history of being uh...

00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:33.960
segregated

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:38.440
uh... so it was uh... it was uh... decided they decided to integrate

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:41.860
uh... i was offered to challenge uh... academically and and and the opportunity

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:43.380
to play in the southwest conference

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:45.950
at that time was insignificant

00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:47.910
here's my dream

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:48.930
go there

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:51.550
and i'm going to do this

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:53.930
and that was my dream and uh...

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.580
everything else is coming

00:46:55.580 --> 00:46:56.760
you know followed me

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.830
you know after i made that decision

00:46:58.830 --> 00:47:00.000
but it but it

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:06.620
It didn't deter me, but also it didn't cause me to feel any differently about myself, like,

00:47:06.740 --> 00:47:08.760
hey, you know, everybody look at me, I'm the first.

00:47:09.400 --> 00:47:11.260
That wasn't the case.

00:47:12.660 --> 00:47:17.460
Some of their teammates at that time were players that were not only standouts on the

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:21.810
field of play, but served their school in leadership roles after their playing days.

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:26.130
All-conference players Bucky Allshouse and Jay Collins are current members of the Rice

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:26.990
Board of Trustees.

00:47:27.510 --> 00:47:30.000
Leland Winston serves as the Alls team physicians.

00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:32.820
Roger Roich was an all-conference standout.

00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:39.860
It was just a great experience and not one thing particularly stands out, camaraderie,

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:43.620
and after I got drafted this is one thing that kind of stands out to me and I went up

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:50.700
to Denver and I was there and you know they were nice guys but they just weren't the caliber

00:47:50.700 --> 00:48:00.000
that I was used to and I don't want to say anything more than that other than I was spoiled.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:06.520
rebounded from a winless 1968 season to win two of his last three in 1969 and set the stage for

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:12.040
optimism in 1970. That optimism provided some cover for growing sentiment about the need for

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:17.950
a change in direction, but the expectation for success in 1970 was clear. However, one of Hagen's

00:48:17.950 --> 00:48:23.390
key components for that success, Barnes, would not be able to be by his coach's side for the 1970

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:30.000
Even without Barnes, the Owls closed with a rush, beating Texas A&M, TCU, and Baylor

00:48:30.090 --> 00:48:35.390
in their final three games to finish at five and five, but it was not enough to save Hagan.

00:48:35.710 --> 00:48:39.990
Despite his key role in advancing the program, Hagan was not retained as head coach,

00:48:40.310 --> 00:48:44.530
and university leadership decided it was necessary to make an investment in the program

00:48:44.530 --> 00:48:47.010
to improve the school's gridiron fortunes.

00:48:47.690 --> 00:48:51.710
That was the year that I think that I would have made a significant difference,

00:48:51.910 --> 00:48:55.660
and I think that we would have been successful,

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:59.770
and probably he would have had a great opportunity to hang on.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:07.550
was a very, very good man. I liked him a lot. He's a good coach. That year I think we would

00:49:07.550 --> 00:49:13.230
have been successful. Construction of a then state-of-the-art end zone training facility

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:18.470
as well as the R room was underway and would open in 1972. And the Owls went across the

00:49:18.470 --> 00:49:24.150
country to Florida State to attract a proven winner in head coach Bill Peterson. Peterson's

00:49:24.150 --> 00:49:30.000
tenure at Rice brought great attention as well as great interest from recruits all over the country who wanted to play in

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:35.140
his NFL-style attack in a conference that seemed to churn out national title contenders each year.

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:39.200
But Peterson's pro-style approach was also a culture shock to many on the team.

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:45.020
On top of it all, Peterson consented to allow a writer total access to the team that year for a

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:50.400
book that would prove to be troubling to members of the Owls. Peterson's arrival also provided the

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:55.520
spark to bring Houston and Rice together on the field for the first time, and a sellout crowd saw

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:00.000
the underdog owls throw a major scare into the Cougars. The season was noted by

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:04.820
the overall performance of an Owls team that battled injuries to keep many games close,

00:50:04.820 --> 00:50:08.380
and saw it close with a 23-0 win over Baylor.

00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:13.000
But ultimately, the Peterson era was marked by the way it ended as suddenly as it began,

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:17.580
when he was hired to take his coaching talents a short drive down South Main, when he was

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:20.580
hired to take over as coach of the Houston Oilers.

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:25.610
"Rice's idea big time was, let's go back to Jess Neely, and I think that's exactly

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:26.610
what they thought.

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:30.000
We may have made a mistake with Bo Hagan, he wasn't in his excitement.

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:35.920
field offense than we thought. He was successful in the field. So let's go out and get a big

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:40.850
name coach. Bill Peterson was a big name coach. Can you imagine getting the coach from Florida

00:50:40.850 --> 00:50:46.340
to take the head job at Rice? And yet he was very attracted to it, thought he could be

00:50:46.340 --> 00:50:51.530
successful, was going to bring the passing game, a whole new era, and a tremendous amount

00:50:51.530 --> 00:50:55.890
of support for the transition at that time. So yes, it was a commitment and it was going

00:50:55.890 --> 00:51:00.000
to be, we're going to do it, we're going back big time, we're going to be successful, and we're going to make

00:51:02.240 --> 00:51:05.880
In 20 years, Rice had gone from a single dominant

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:08.700
entertainment option to being one of many options

00:51:08.700 --> 00:51:12.130
in a town that continued to grow on a world stage.

00:51:12.130 --> 00:51:15.430
In the next 20 years, Rice would become more challenged

00:51:15.430 --> 00:51:17.750
to keep up with its Southwest Conference mates

00:51:17.750 --> 00:51:19.750
and keep itself in front of a growing

00:51:19.750 --> 00:51:21.850
and diverse local population,

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:24.750
while never straying from its academic mission.

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:27.580
The results would be varied, but at the end of the journey,

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:30.000
Rice stood poised to once again prove

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:31.740
that athletics and academics

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:33.840
need not be conflicting elements,

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:36.520
but complementary parts of a worthy mission.

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:00.000
The seniors who made up the 19th

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.240
1972 Rysel's football team enter the season confronted by perpetual change.

00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:08.960
They enter their final fall camp under the direction of their third head coach in as

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:13.520
many years and found the depth chart crowded by an incoming class of talented freshmen.

00:52:13.520 --> 00:52:18.100
The result of an aggressive national effort by departed head coach Bill Peterson.

00:52:18.100 --> 00:52:22.600
Thanks to a change of NCAA rules, these yearlings stepped onto the field for the first day of

00:52:22.600 --> 00:52:25.240
practice with unlimited expectations.

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:29.200
None of the veterans could bank on the kind of seniority normally afforded players of

00:52:29.200 --> 00:52:29.920
their rank.

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:34.860
Peterson's surprising arrival one year earlier had been a jolt to the status quo on South

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:39.760
Main, so his equally surprising departure left a program once defined by the stability

00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:41.560
of its coaching regime shaken.

00:52:41.890 --> 00:52:46.480
Players who had bought into the adoption of a pro-style attack under Peterson or were

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:51.140
recruited for the same system found they need not worry about a radical shift in the playbook

00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:56.090
when the Owls elevated Al Conover, who had been one of Peterson's top assistants to the

00:52:56.090 --> 00:52:57.050
head coaching position.

00:52:57.410 --> 00:53:00.000
Known for his sharp offensive mind as well as a quick win.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:04.680
wit and a flair for the dramatic, Conover was well-equipped to hone the assembled talent

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:08.420
as well as assume the role as the face of the program.

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:16.350
I had a great bunch of players here, guys that I was attached to, and they wanted to

00:53:16.350 --> 00:53:23.050
keep the system, you know, they liked the pro passing game and so on, and I kept some

00:53:23.050 --> 00:53:30.000
of the staff and then brought in some others, but by and large it made the transition.

00:53:30.160 --> 00:53:36.080
His debut proved to be one that few Rice coaches could ever hope to top.

00:53:36.080 --> 00:53:42.300
A crowd of 52,000 gathered at Rice Stadium to witness Conover's Owls debut against Houston.

00:53:42.720 --> 00:53:48.020
With the knowledge that the United States had lost the gold medal in Olympic basketball earlier the same day,

00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:51.500
they should have known they were in for a wild finish of their own.

00:53:51.900 --> 00:53:54.940
Holding a 14-13 lead in the game's final moments,

00:53:55.260 --> 00:54:00.000
the Owls were forced to withstand one final charge by Houston, stopping them at the goal line.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.440
as time expired.

00:54:02.560 --> 00:54:04.180
Bill Yeoman, the coach then

00:54:04.180 --> 00:54:06.440
at U of H

00:54:06.440 --> 00:54:08.420
decides instead of kicking

00:54:08.420 --> 00:54:10.410
a field goal, they're

00:54:10.410 --> 00:54:12.330
going to just run it off, run their

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:13.830
veer and score.

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:17.030
And the Rice

00:54:17.030 --> 00:54:19.110
defense just stuffed

00:54:19.110 --> 00:54:21.130
it and we won the game.

00:54:21.780 --> 00:54:23.200
And I think I hit my

00:54:23.200 --> 00:54:25.120
head on the ceiling on a water

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:26.880
pipe jumping up in the locker room

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:27.560
and

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:29.570
I

00:54:30.890 --> 00:54:33.130
The Owls would beat Clemson the following week

00:54:33.130 --> 00:54:36.330
and then tie Georgia Tech to keep Conover unbeaten

00:54:36.330 --> 00:54:37.890
in its first three games.

00:54:37.890 --> 00:54:41.030
The Owls would endure a mid-season four-game losing streak

00:54:41.030 --> 00:54:43.070
before winning three of the last four

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:47.350
to finish 5-5-1, their best mark in nine years.

00:54:47.350 --> 00:54:50.460
One year later, Conover posted the Owls' first winning record

00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:53.460
in the Southwest Conference since 1963,

00:54:53.460 --> 00:54:56.820
thanks to a four-game winning streak to end the year.

00:54:56.820 --> 00:55:00.000
Conover's teams could count on a number of dynamic individual

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:05.240
talents whose football careers would extend beyond their days on South Main. Linebacker

00:55:05.240 --> 00:55:10.480
Rodrigo Barnes was a nightmare for opposing coaches, combining size and speed that allowed

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:15.450
him to dominate from a variety of spots on the field before moving on to an NFL career

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:20.050
that included a Super Bowl title with the Oakland Raiders. Tight end Gary Butler was

00:55:20.050 --> 00:55:24.170
a two-time All-Southwest Conference selection and a second-round pick of the Kansas City

00:55:24.170 --> 00:55:29.770
Chiefs. Defensive back Bruce Henley and defensive lineman Cornelius Walker were also standouts.

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:32.280
The Conover era's flair for the dramatic

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:34.240
showed up often on special teams.

00:55:34.240 --> 00:55:38.120
The Owls returned three kickoffs for touchdowns in 1973,

00:55:38.120 --> 00:55:40.800
but none were more dramatic than Carl Swerke's

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:43.440
long-distance shocker with two minutes left

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:47.180
that led the Owls to an upset win over Texas A&M.

00:55:47.180 --> 00:55:48.620
Despite his best efforts,

00:55:48.620 --> 00:55:51.380
Conover could not sustain his early momentum,

00:55:51.380 --> 00:55:54.430
and in 1976, Rice continued to look

00:55:54.430 --> 00:55:57.750
for more offensive explosiveness on that side of the ball.

00:55:57.750 --> 00:56:00.000
Homer Rice had been a respected developer

00:56:00.030 --> 00:56:03.690
quarterbacks who had been a prime candidate when Conover was hired. He came

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:07.410
to Rice determined to turn the run dominated Southwest Conference on its

00:56:07.410 --> 00:56:11.730
ear, a desire that was only heightened by the presence of a gunslinger in the

00:56:11.730 --> 00:56:16.900
making already on the Rice campus. Tommy Kramer had come to Rice as one of the

00:56:16.900 --> 00:56:20.920
more heralded throwing quarterbacks in Texas high school history. In his first

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:25.120
three seasons, he had shown flashes of brilliance, but it wasn't until Homer

00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:29.140
Rice had witnessed him firsthand during spring practice that he knew he had the

00:56:29.140 --> 00:56:30.000
perfect candidate

00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:31.060
for the job.

00:56:31.060 --> 00:56:36.910
He says, but after watching film of you, he says, I'll tell you what, he says, you're

00:56:36.910 --> 00:56:43.220
this far away from being an All-American quarterback and a first-round pick, and he says, you follow

00:56:43.220 --> 00:56:46.720
these drills that I give you, and he says, I'll guarantee you, you'll be a first-round

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:48.220
pick.

00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:50.540
I said, that's good enough for me.

00:56:50.540 --> 00:56:55.450
From the first snap of the 1976 season, the Rice Owls filled the air with leather in a

00:56:55.450 --> 00:56:59.170
manner never before seen in the history of the Southwest Conference.

00:56:59.170 --> 00:57:00.000
Kramer threw for three.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:05.800
319 yards and four touchdowns in the opener against Utah and kept firing each week until

00:57:05.800 --> 00:57:11.200
he became the first conference passer to top 3,000 yards in a season, finishing with

00:57:11.200 --> 00:57:16.660
3,317, the second highest total in NCAA history at that time.

00:57:17.090 --> 00:57:21.950
He compiled four of the top eight single-game totals in conference history, finishing with

00:57:21.950 --> 00:57:25.490
a 409-yard effort in the season finale against Houston.

00:57:25.490 --> 00:57:30.000
I can read the papers every week and, you know, see where I'm at in the standing

00:57:30.890 --> 00:57:36.740
passing and all that, and I pretty much led the nation throughout the year in yardage

00:57:36.740 --> 00:57:44.310
and touchdown passes, so I had a sense of what it was, but that wasn't going to keep

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:46.950
me from working harder.

00:57:46.950 --> 00:57:52.150
Despite the Owls' 3-8 record, Kramer earned consensus All-American honors and finished

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:54.640
fifth in the voting for the Heisman Trophy.

00:57:54.640 --> 00:58:00.000
Kramer and his receivers, including David Hauser, established passing records that would stand

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:05.240
30 years, but lightning would not strike twice for Homer Rice, who could not find a replacement

00:58:05.240 --> 00:58:11.100
capable of running his offense as Kramer had done during the year of the Rice Air Corps.

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:15.800
Homer Rice's tenure began a stretch of 13 years in which the Owls were guided by four

00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:22.070
different head coaches. Ray Alborn, who had joined the Owl staff in 1972 under Al Conover,

00:58:22.070 --> 00:58:26.290
became the only Rice graduate to assume the role of head football coach when he succeeded

00:58:26.290 --> 00:58:30.000
Homer Rice in 1978. He would have the longest tenure of the

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:36.360
His best season came in 1980 when he led the Owls to a 5-6 overall record and a 4-4 mark

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:37.940
in the Southwest Conference.

00:58:37.940 --> 00:58:44.040
All four wins came on the road, the last a 35-7 stunner over Houston in the Astrodome.

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:48.700
The following year, Alborn was 3-5 in the Southwest Conference, but a loss in the finale

00:58:48.700 --> 00:58:55.320
to Houston started a 17-game conference losing streak that ended Alborn's time on the sidelines

00:58:55.320 --> 00:58:59.030
after the 1983 season.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:09.470
along as the momentum of losing had kind of taken root here and the recruiting was getting

00:59:09.470 --> 00:59:12.910
harder and the schedule was getting tougher.

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:21.630
So I think it was just a situation where coaching couldn't turn it around.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:27.740
Alborn was followed by Watson Brown and Jerry Burns, who each found only modest success in

00:59:27.740 --> 00:59:30.000
their tenures on the South Main sideline.

00:59:30.060 --> 00:59:34.500
Despite the modest win totals, there were individual talents wearing the blue and gray,

00:59:34.780 --> 00:59:37.060
a fact borne out by their play in the NFL.

00:59:37.660 --> 00:59:41.920
Earl Cooper and Daryl Grant went on to long and successful NFL careers,

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:47.060
including Super Bowl rings with the San Francisco 49ers and Washington Redskins, respectively.

00:59:47.520 --> 00:59:51.620
Safety Michael Downs was a key member of the Dallas Cowboys' doomsday defense.

00:59:52.070 --> 00:59:55.350
Two-way player Donald Hollis went on to play quarterback in the NFL.

00:59:55.710 --> 00:59:58.180
Steve Kidd earned All-American honors,

00:59:58.180 --> 01:00:00.000
and Quentin Roper and Courtney Hall

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:04.980
were all conference performers at their positions. Coach Auburn and the staff did

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:14.350
enough for us to compete at a high level and put us in position that if we was going to

01:00:14.350 --> 01:00:19.810
stand out, we would get ready for the next level. In the wake of the departure of Jerry

01:00:19.810 --> 01:00:24.280
Burnt as head coach, the list of applicants knew full well the challenge that awaited

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:30.000
them in Houston. A program that had once frequently flown in the national top ten had now gone

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:32.840
25 years without a season over 500.

01:00:33.300 --> 01:00:35.800
The Owls needed a spark of energy and confidence,

01:00:36.120 --> 01:00:39.100
and it came from someone whose defenses had never allowed

01:00:39.100 --> 01:00:42.900
more than two touchdowns by the Owls in his five years at Arkansas.

01:00:43.390 --> 01:00:46.180
Fred Goldsmith had once stymied Owls' hopes,

01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:50.240
but now those same coaching skills, fueled by a tireless energy

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:53.340
and fronted by a winning smile and engaging personality,

01:00:53.760 --> 01:00:55.790
soon were causing recruits throughout the region

01:00:55.790 --> 01:00:59.490
to reconsider their initial hesitation about casting their lot

01:00:59.490 --> 01:01:00.000
with the outline.

01:01:00.640 --> 01:01:05.600
Goldsmith's debut as the Owls head coach against SMU was somewhat overshadowed by the fact

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:09.640
that it was the Mustangs' first game back after the NCAA's death penalty, and on the

01:01:09.640 --> 01:01:14.440
surface a 2-8-1 finish might not have been the indicator some had hoped.

01:01:14.440 --> 01:01:19.740
But a late season 6-3 win at Baylor, in which the Owls showcased the same defensive tenacity

01:01:19.740 --> 01:01:24.640
that was the cornerstone of Goldsmith's Arkansas teams, would prove to be a turning point in

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:26.170
the eyes of many.

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:30.000
The 1990 season saw the Owls introduce a distinctive new look.

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:35.280
on the field, switching to a blue helmet with owl wings on each side. The new look was accompanied

01:01:35.280 --> 01:01:41.200
by a major promotional push announcing that the Rice Owls had a new set of wings. Such optimism

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:46.480
was fueled by the combination by a solid group of seniors and a talented group of underclassmen

01:01:46.480 --> 01:01:51.720
who were part of Goldsmith's first recruiting hall and had gained valuable experience in 1989.

01:01:52.360 --> 01:01:57.090
Senior quarterback Donald Hollis had established himself as a threat with both his arms and legs,

01:01:57.250 --> 01:02:00.000
a threat that was bolstered by an exciting talent at running

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:05.720
back in sophomore Trevor Cobb. Cobb had burst onto the scene, setting a freshman rushing record

01:02:05.720 --> 01:02:11.800
in 1989. On the defensive side, sophomore nose guard Matt Sine led an attacking swarm that was

01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:17.420
relentless and inspired by the relentless play and leadership of undersized linebacker O.J.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:22.720
Brigance. Rice opened the year winning two of three non-conference games, gave Goldsmith a

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:29.130
triumphant return to Fayetteville with a 19-11 win, and after a 30-28 win over SMU, the Owls

01:02:29.130 --> 01:02:30.000
were poised to post

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:35.400
their first winning season since 1963 before falling to Baylor by a single

01:02:35.400 --> 01:02:40.220
point. We played hard against Texas. We played hard against Houston. It never

01:02:40.220 --> 01:02:45.410
fell our way. It's hard to turn things around and Fred really believed and

01:02:45.410 --> 01:02:50.990
we believed in Fred and it turned out great for us. Brigance would leave

01:02:50.990 --> 01:02:55.580
Rice as the school's all-time leader in tackles and a two-time All-Southwest

01:02:55.580 --> 01:03:00.000
Conference selection. He would prove doubting pro scouts wrong and eventually play on the

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:01.840
Baltimore Ravens Super Bowl championship

01:03:01.840 --> 01:03:03.640
team. While he left campus in

01:03:03.640 --> 01:03:05.900
1990, his impact was not lost

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:07.820
on those who assumed leadership in

01:03:07.820 --> 01:03:10.440
his absence. O.J. was very

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:12.460
intense. Practice, off

01:03:12.460 --> 01:03:14.070
the field, during the game

01:03:14.070 --> 01:03:16.240
and he had an approach

01:03:16.240 --> 01:03:18.240
to the game that everyone

01:03:18.240 --> 01:03:20.240
wanted to copy. You knew

01:03:20.240 --> 01:03:21.760
he was going to be destined for greatness

01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:24.060
in whatever he chose, whether it was football

01:03:24.060 --> 01:03:26.170
or business, you knew he was destined for

01:03:26.170 --> 01:03:28.290
greatness because first of all, he was going to outwork

01:03:28.290 --> 01:03:30.000
everybody and he was going to outsmart

01:03:31.240 --> 01:03:37.510
He was someone you wanted on your side, and just a fine man for sure.

01:03:37.510 --> 01:03:42.430
Driven by the knowledge that a winning season had come so close, the 1991 Owls took to the

01:03:42.430 --> 01:03:44.790
field looking to complete the mission.

01:03:44.790 --> 01:03:48.840
While that goal would have to wait one more year, the '91 campaign would have its own

01:03:48.840 --> 01:03:53.920
claim to fame, thanks to the fast feet and darting running style of Trevor Cobb.

01:03:53.920 --> 01:04:00.000
The junior running back tormented opponents, gashing them for nearly 1,700 yards on the

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:04.580
Bob became the first owl to step to the podium at a national awards ceremony when he was

01:04:04.580 --> 01:04:07.880
named the winner of the Doak Walker Award.

01:04:07.880 --> 01:04:12.580
In a tumultuous 20 years, Rice had seen its foundation shaken by a series of coaching

01:04:12.580 --> 01:04:17.400
changes, each in search of the elusive chemistry to return the owls to their previous level

01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:18.580
of success.

01:04:18.580 --> 01:04:23.910
In those 20 years, a pair of owls had fashioned individual seasons that continued to withstand

01:04:23.910 --> 01:04:28.910
the test of time, while others moved on to create memorable moments in the NFL.

01:04:28.910 --> 01:04:30.000
At the same time,

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:35.180
countless others have leveraged their competitive experiences into successful post-football

01:04:35.180 --> 01:04:41.020
ventures, but all remain united in one goal: to see Rice once again celebrating victories

01:04:41.020 --> 01:04:43.320
on repeated Saturdays in the fall.

01:04:43.320 --> 01:04:48.980
The next 20 years would provide emphatic emphasis that such a pursuit was not only attainable,

01:04:48.980 --> 01:04:53.560
but essential to broadening the image of Rice to an even wider audience.

01:05:16.860 --> 01:05:20.220
In the three years since he had first stepped onto the Rice campus,

01:05:20.220 --> 01:05:23.630
Fred Goldsmith had brought the Owls tantalizingly close

01:05:23.630 --> 01:05:27.170
to ending the drought that had seen a generation of Rice Owls fans

01:05:27.170 --> 01:05:30.000
and graduates not experience a winning season.

01:05:30.120 --> 01:05:32.400
But high hopes for the 1992 season

01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:35.680
were quickly challenged after losses to Air Force and Duke

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:36.840
to open the year.

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:39.900
Two weeks later, a heartbreaking 23-21 loss

01:05:39.900 --> 01:05:42.240
to Texas in the Southwest Conference opener

01:05:42.240 --> 01:05:44.300
left the Owls at 1-3.

01:05:44.300 --> 01:05:46.800
But Goldsmith would not let his Owls fall victim

01:05:46.800 --> 01:05:49.390
to the apparently dashed expectations.

01:05:49.390 --> 01:05:52.270
The Owls righted the ship to win four of the next five

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:55.220
conference games, setting the stage for a non-conference

01:05:55.220 --> 01:05:58.280
battle with Navy at Rice Stadium in late November

01:05:58.280 --> 01:06:00.000
with a winning season just six

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:06.940
It really felt like by the time we got to 92, and early on, it was tough early on.

01:06:06.940 --> 01:06:11.680
We lost the first two games, but we really felt like we were a really good team, and

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:15.080
that was the year to get the thing done, and we got it done.

01:06:15.080 --> 01:06:19.400
The following year, it again came down to the final home game of the season to secure

01:06:19.400 --> 01:06:24.680
a winning campaign, and the Owls again delivered, dispatching their crosstown rivals, Houston,

01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:26.500
37-7.

01:06:26.500 --> 01:06:30.000
The win brought the Bayou Bucket back to Rice for only the fourth time.

01:06:30.060 --> 01:06:32.900
in the 20 years since the Touchdown Club of Houston

01:06:32.900 --> 01:06:36.060
had created it as a symbol of victory in the game.

01:06:36.530 --> 01:06:38.030
Few realized it at the time,

01:06:38.250 --> 01:06:40.650
but the Owls' resounding victory that November day

01:06:40.650 --> 01:06:43.990
would draw the curtain on the one-sided nature of the series.

01:06:44.270 --> 01:06:46.770
With Rice winning seven of the next 15,

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:49.330
the bucket became a true rivalry game,

01:06:49.550 --> 01:06:53.470
a celebration of college football in Houston for all to enjoy.

01:06:53.960 --> 01:06:56.140
Fred was a great football coach,

01:06:57.750 --> 01:07:00.000
you know, a great defensive specialist,

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:08.460
I think he had the support of the university, and all things considered did a great job

01:07:08.460 --> 01:07:14.640
of beginning to get the ball rolling with two winning seasons.

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:19.400
After guiding the Owls to back-to-back winning campaigns, Fred Goldsmith decided to accept

01:07:19.400 --> 01:07:24.400
the head coaching position at Duke, leaving behind an opportunity far more realized than

01:07:24.400 --> 01:07:26.840
it had been for many of his predecessors.

01:07:26.840 --> 01:07:30.000
The irony was that the man who was selected to follow in Goldsmith's name was the first

01:07:30.060 --> 01:07:36.330
Smith's footsteps had been his boss at Arkansas before he came to rice. Ken Hatfield had been a

01:07:36.330 --> 01:07:41.070
winner at every stop of his coaching career, fashioning winners at Air Force and Arkansas

01:07:41.070 --> 01:07:45.910
before moving on to Clemson, where despite restoring the honor of a program that had

01:07:45.910 --> 01:07:51.050
been tarnished by scandal, he could never win the favor of his fan base. Goldsmith's departure

01:07:51.050 --> 01:07:56.130
provided Hatfield the chance to come back to a conference he knew well, and he jumped at the

01:07:56.130 --> 01:07:58.220
chance. Having coached

01:07:58.220 --> 01:08:00.000
against them here in 84 through 89,

01:08:00.440 --> 01:08:06.110
was at Arkansas during that time, it was always our toughest game. For whatever reason, Rice,

01:08:06.110 --> 01:08:10.270
just where they came in the schedule, the way they played, they always had good solid

01:08:10.270 --> 01:08:16.700
coaching. It always was a very tough game. We liked the people and liked the situation,

01:08:16.700 --> 01:08:19.650
and so we decided to go to Rice.

01:08:19.650 --> 01:08:23.830
Hatfield knew his return to the Southwest Conference was going to be short-lived, as

01:08:23.830 --> 01:08:30.000
in March of 1994, four members of the league, Texas, Texas A&M, Texas Tech, and Baylor

01:08:30.160 --> 01:08:35.800
announced they were abandoning the conference that had defined Texas football for nearly 80 years.

01:08:36.350 --> 01:08:42.510
As short-lived as it was, Hatfield's return to the Southwest Conference would give the Yals one more moment to savor.

01:08:43.140 --> 01:08:49.200
A proponent of an option-based rushing attack, dependent less on individual talent than on a cohesive unit,

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:55.360
Hatfield had flourished at Air Force and his style had been a counterpoint to the growing move towards passing attacks

01:08:55.360 --> 01:08:58.400
among his conference rivals at Arkansas and Clemson.

01:08:58.400 --> 01:09:00.000
THE EARLY RESULTS IN 1990

01:09:00.030 --> 01:09:05.490
1994 were not encouraging as the Owls dropped games with Tulane and Kansas State to open the

01:09:05.490 --> 01:09:11.390
year. But wins over Iowa State and Texas Tech evened the Owls marked a 2-2 as Rice prepared

01:09:11.390 --> 01:09:17.560
to meet an old rival on a unique national stage. Major League Baseball season had come to a

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:23.440
premature end in August of 1994 when the players went on strike. The major sports networks were

01:09:23.440 --> 01:09:29.130
in a mad scramble to replace programming and ESPN found itself with a huge hole in its Sunday

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:30.000
night schedule.

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:35.100
Into the void stepped the Southwest Conference, which offered up the Rice-Texas matchup, and

01:09:35.100 --> 01:09:36.520
ESPN accepted.

01:09:36.520 --> 01:09:41.800
It turned out to be big because all the nation got a chance to see the game, and I think

01:09:41.800 --> 01:09:46.830
that really exposed the nation to Rice University and many opportunities you have on television

01:09:46.830 --> 01:09:51.510
to sell your program, the university itself, so I think it turned out to be a positive

01:09:51.510 --> 01:09:53.330
thing.

01:09:53.330 --> 01:09:58.180
On a rainy night, one that suited Hatfield's ball-control style more than the wide-open

01:09:58.180 --> 01:10:00.000
attack that 13th-ranked Texas U.S.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:05.140
employed an inspired group of owls muted the burnt orange in the crowd jumping out to a quick

01:10:05.140 --> 01:10:11.370
nine nothing lead texas briefly found its footing to take a 10-9 edge but a second field goal by

01:10:11.370 --> 01:10:16.850
matt hulesman gave the yells the lead they would never relinquish we were up before they even

01:10:16.850 --> 01:10:21.890
touched the ball we're up over two scores and uh and but we fought our defense continued to fight

01:10:21.890 --> 01:10:26.940
hard all the time and and really they scored on a touchdown pass it was uh maybe a little

01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:30.000
offensive interference on their part right here you know we thought it was

01:10:30.090 --> 01:10:35.700
anyway but they did score. Other than that our defense continued to not let them have any kind

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:42.780
of drives or momentum. A touchdown pass from Josh LaRocca to Spencer George extended the lead to 19-10

01:10:42.780 --> 01:10:48.520
and an inspired defensive effort led by N.D. Kalou and Larry Izzo continued to thwart the

01:10:48.520 --> 01:10:53.370
horns at every turn but in keeping with the history of the series the final outcome was in

01:10:53.370 --> 01:10:59.170
doubt until the Owls recovered a last-ditch onside kick attempt to seal the first win over Texas

01:10:59.170 --> 01:11:00.000
since 1960.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.400
1965, creating a legion of fans who claim to have been in the stands that night that

01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:08.340
far exceeds the actual attendance of 34,000.

01:11:08.660 --> 01:11:13.420
The following week, the Owls were blanked by Texas A&M, but the parody in the conference

01:11:13.420 --> 01:11:17.500
that season kept the Owls in the race, and they earned a share of the Southwest Conference

01:11:17.500 --> 01:11:22.220
title on the final weekend of the season by posting their second straight Bayou Bucket

01:11:22.220 --> 01:11:23.500
win over Houston.

01:11:24.030 --> 01:11:29.370
That spirited rivalry and Rice's newfound dominance of the series were another casualty

01:11:29.370 --> 01:11:30.000
of the end of the season.

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:35.070
Southwest Conference, the Cougars joined Conference USA, and the burgeoning rivalry went on a

01:11:35.070 --> 01:11:39.210
three-year hiatus until a non-conference date for both schools was available.

01:11:39.670 --> 01:11:42.030
Eventually it got to, you keep your gate.

01:11:42.400 --> 01:11:47.100
That was sort of the trend where it was like, well, everybody, it's not a homogeneous group

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:53.760
and things need to change because the big boys wanted to see things change.

01:11:53.760 --> 01:12:00.000
We understand what was happening at that time, and I think that we had allowed the programs

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:05.120
go down and so hopefully we learn the lesson and we're going to learn about it and we're

01:12:05.120 --> 01:12:10.200
going to build it bigger and better and ultimately that's what we're going to do.

01:12:10.200 --> 01:12:15.100
Two years later, Hatfield had populated his ranks with players ideally suited to his option

01:12:15.100 --> 01:12:16.100
attack.

01:12:16.100 --> 01:12:19.760
The Owls' entry into the Western Athletic Conference brought Owls fans a new set of

01:12:19.760 --> 01:12:22.640
teams and a new set of travel destinations.

01:12:22.640 --> 01:12:28.600
It also brought them their first seven-win season since 1961 as the Owls won six of their

01:12:28.600 --> 01:12:30.000
last seven WAC games.

01:12:30.700 --> 01:12:33.500
One year later, the Owls again won seven games,

01:12:33.500 --> 01:12:38.400
their first back-to-back seasons with that win total since 1960-61.

01:12:38.400 --> 01:12:42.300
And Owl fans began to get accustomed to the wide-open brand of football

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:44.400
that had made the WAC legendary.

01:12:44.400 --> 01:12:47.500
They also won a shootout over a Northwestern squad

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:50.250
that had won the Big Ten title the year before.

01:12:50.250 --> 01:12:53.850
The Bayou Bucket returned to the schedule in 1999

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:56.660
and, in a nod to the earliest days of the series,

01:12:56.660 --> 01:13:00.000
became the season opener for Houston and Rice.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:05.240
2000, the schools kicked the year off in rousing fashion with the Owls winning in overtime on a

01:13:05.240 --> 01:13:12.210
short run by Robbie Beck. The 2001 season was one of anticipation and promise for Owls fans and that

01:13:12.210 --> 01:13:17.390
optimism was validated by a second straight win over Houston in the opener and a two-point win

01:13:17.390 --> 01:13:22.540
at Duke. The next game on the schedule was a formidable assignment, a date against Nebraska

01:13:22.540 --> 01:13:28.140
in Lincoln. But as Hatfield was interviewed on Tuesday morning of that week, events were unfolding

01:13:28.140 --> 01:13:30.000
on the television that would render

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:35.780
concerns about the strength of a football opponent moot. As the nation paused to reflect upon the

01:13:35.780 --> 01:13:40.620
events in New York and their implications, Hatfield and his staff knew that while football

01:13:40.620 --> 01:13:45.980
was not an immediate priority, it would rapidly become an agent of helping the country return to

01:13:45.980 --> 01:13:51.310
some sense of normalcy. Since both Nebraska and Rice had open dates the following week,

01:13:51.410 --> 01:13:55.750
it was determined that their game would be moved to the following Thursday, and the Owls,

01:13:55.750 --> 01:14:00.000
as the visitor would be one of the first sports teams to board an airliner at

01:14:00.060 --> 01:14:04.320
the attacks. It was a story of national interest and the Owls found themselves

01:14:04.320 --> 01:14:09.830
with a much larger traveling party than usual. ESPN came down, they had a big

01:14:09.830 --> 01:14:14.160
documentary on us and we had a lot of national exposure and that was a very

01:14:14.160 --> 01:14:18.540
emotional moment because we were the first team to play in America, all eyes

01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:24.110
were on us, they had the flyovers, it was very patriotic, so it was a turning

01:14:24.110 --> 01:14:30.000
point in our country and we had national exposure during that time. After losing to the

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:34.980
Nebraska, the Owls ripped off three straight wins before seeing an amazing comeback at

01:14:34.980 --> 01:14:40.640
Louisiana Tech fall short in overtime. A loss to David Carr and Fresno State followed. But then

01:14:40.640 --> 01:14:46.840
wins over Tulsa and UTEP pushed the Owls' win total to eight, their highest total since 1953.

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:51.820
It's a win total that football fans today would associate as guaranteeing a bowl berth.

01:14:51.960 --> 01:14:57.380
But there were far fewer bowls in 2001, and the WAC in particular had few guaranteed slots

01:14:57.380 --> 01:14:58.240
available.

01:14:58.240 --> 01:15:00.000
But the gals were not alone in sitting

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:05.300
home that Christmas with a stellar win total. Over the next four years, Hatfield's option

01:15:05.300 --> 01:15:10.720
attack proved to be a potent foe for WAC defenders, but injuries to key components stalled their

01:15:10.720 --> 01:15:14.890
efforts to challenge for the league title. Despite the struggles, there were moments

01:15:14.890 --> 01:15:20.360
to savor. In 2004, the Owls fashioned one of their greatest defensive efforts in its

01:15:20.360 --> 01:15:25.030
history, holding Houston off the scoreboard until the final seconds of the game. Defensive

01:15:25.030 --> 01:15:30.000
Then John Siptak and his teammates harassed Houston quarterback Kevin Cobb into submission,

01:15:30.160 --> 01:15:34.520
new Rice President David Lebron the thrill of capturing the Bayou Bucket in his first

01:15:34.520 --> 01:15:36.560
year on South Main.

01:15:36.560 --> 01:15:42.360
In 2005, in an effort to reconnect with traditional Southwest Conference foes such as SMU, TCU

01:15:42.360 --> 01:15:48.000
and Houston, as well as lessen some of the economic burdens of the far-flung WAC configuration,

01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:52.280
Rice said goodbye to the Western Athletic Conference and became a member of Conference

01:15:52.280 --> 01:15:53.270
USA.

01:15:53.270 --> 01:16:00.000
The 2005 Owls found the going in their initial trip through Conference USA a difficult one.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:04.660
win over Tulane, serving as the final victory of Ken Hatfield's career.

01:16:04.660 --> 01:16:09.540
The veteran coach, who had led the Owls into competition in three conferences, announced

01:16:09.540 --> 01:16:13.200
the end of his tenure shortly after the end of the season.

01:16:13.200 --> 01:16:17.760
To replace Hatfield, the Owls settled on a fiery young defensive coordinator who had

01:16:17.760 --> 01:16:20.270
helped lead Tulsa to a lead title.

01:16:20.270 --> 01:16:25.030
Todd Graham was an abrupt departure from Hatfield, a fact that was evident from the first moments

01:16:25.030 --> 01:16:26.400
he stepped onto campus.

01:16:26.400 --> 01:16:29.640
Todd was an outstanding assistant at Tulsa.

01:16:30.830 --> 01:16:34.310
had great credentials, great recommendations

01:16:34.310 --> 01:16:37.700
from people that I had great respect for.

01:16:37.700 --> 01:16:42.540
And he came in facing one of the toughest schedules,

01:16:42.540 --> 01:16:45.970
non-conference schedules, maybe in recorded history.

01:16:47.910 --> 01:16:51.490
And that didn't deter him in the least.

01:16:51.490 --> 01:16:53.230
- While his specialty was defense,

01:16:53.230 --> 01:16:55.630
Graham sought to bring the same kind of wide open

01:16:55.630 --> 01:16:58.960
offensive style that was sweeping the country to South Maine.

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:00.000
- To lead that charge,

01:17:00.060 --> 01:17:04.940
He brought a familiar name to Texas football fans who was just beginning his coaching career.

01:17:05.410 --> 01:17:09.210
Major Applewhite had built a career that was on par with his name.

01:17:09.210 --> 01:17:14.090
And his hiring ignited even greater excitement surrounding the 2006 season.

01:17:14.630 --> 01:17:21.030
As with countless other coaches, Graham and Applewhite knew that the best of intentions for a change of offensive attack

01:17:21.030 --> 01:17:24.740
were solely dependent on the cast of available talent.

01:17:25.290 --> 01:17:30.000
the surface the likelihood that a suitable trigger man and suitable receivers were in place

01:17:30.480 --> 01:17:36.180
seemed far-fetched. But they were in place. Chase Clement and Jarrett Dillard had hardly

01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:40.620
moved the recruiting needle when they were seniors in San Antonio. The offer from Rice

01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:45.480
was among their few, and after signing day, they met for the first time since they faced

01:17:45.480 --> 01:17:51.670
off in a freshman football game. The two quickly began to develop a bond. It took Applewhite

01:17:51.670 --> 01:17:56.670
no time at all to realize that his search for skill position talent was far less formidable

01:17:56.670 --> 01:18:00.000
than he first thought. Al's opponents would prove to be a far

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:05.560
less quick study setting the stage for an improbable 2006 season for the third

01:18:05.560 --> 01:18:10.380
time in history a Rice head coach made his debut against Houston and for the

01:18:10.380 --> 01:18:14.930
third straight time the Owls were the underdog Graham's impact on his team had

01:18:14.930 --> 01:18:19.070
not been a factor for the oddsmakers to consider but the Owls survived a 14

01:18:19.070 --> 01:18:23.790
nothing start by Houston to score 30 consecutive points by early in the third

01:18:23.790 --> 01:18:27.700
quarter Clement was proving to be a perfect fit for the new offensive scheme

01:18:27.700 --> 01:18:30.000
while Dillard and running back Quentin Smith

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:32.890
were creating headaches for defenders.

01:18:32.890 --> 01:18:35.970
Houston stormed back to take a 31-30 lead,

01:18:35.970 --> 01:18:39.210
and an injured Clement tried to rally the Owls one last time

01:18:39.210 --> 01:18:41.050
before coming up just short.

01:18:41.050 --> 01:18:43.650
It was a thrilling beginning that whetted the appetite

01:18:43.650 --> 01:18:46.890
of Owls fans, but a murderous slate of non-conference games

01:18:46.890 --> 01:18:49.530
made it difficult for most to gauge the true abilities

01:18:49.530 --> 01:18:50.310
of this team.

01:18:50.310 --> 01:18:54.330
Consecutive losses to UCLA, Texas, and Florida State

01:18:54.330 --> 01:18:56.590
left the Owls 0-4.

01:18:56.590 --> 01:18:59.730
Clement was set to return the next week for a trip to Army.

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:05.000
Just as they began preparations for that game, this group of owls would find themselves challenged

01:19:05.000 --> 01:19:10.400
in a way no team ever expects to be when freshman defensive back Dale Lloyd collapsed

01:19:10.400 --> 01:19:12.200
and later died after a workout.

01:19:12.200 --> 01:19:15.000
To be honest with you, I just don't remember preparing that week.

01:19:15.000 --> 01:19:19.920
I just remember everything and being focused about Dale and us reevaluating some things

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:26.310
and remembering that, you know, it brought us closer as a team, you know, to know that

01:19:26.310 --> 01:19:29.800
it's the guys next to you in this locker that are important.

01:19:32.650 --> 01:19:38.130
brothers. The stunned team closed ranks and took the field at West Point on the

01:19:38.130 --> 01:19:41.970
day before Lloyd's funeral and fashioned a performance few could have

01:19:41.970 --> 01:19:47.470
anticipated, shredding the cadets 48-14 as Clement returned to action by throwing

01:19:47.470 --> 01:19:52.210
five touchdowns, three of them to Dillard. It would be a combination that was

01:19:52.210 --> 01:19:56.360
repeated over and over in the coming weeks as the Owls fashioned a series of

01:19:56.360 --> 01:20:00.000
improbable victories to finish the season seven and five

01:20:00.120 --> 01:20:07.960
And, thanks to Conference USA's multiple bowl affiliations, present Owl fans with their first bowl berth in 45 years.

01:20:08.260 --> 01:20:17.430
Dillard set an NCAA record by catching a touchdown pass in all 13 games, rising from anonymity to become a finalist for the Boletnikoff Award.

01:20:17.840 --> 01:20:24.000
The Owls' turnaround had become one of the top stories in college football, and Rice fans look forward to the next chapter.

01:20:24.330 --> 01:20:26.770
But what they found instead was that for the second time

01:20:26.770 --> 01:20:30.000
in their history, they would see a head coach leave the program

01:20:30.160 --> 01:20:31.380
after just one season.

01:20:31.870 --> 01:20:35.530
Graham, who had agreed to a contract extension three days earlier,

01:20:35.850 --> 01:20:38.550
abruptly left to return to Tulsa as head coach,

01:20:38.810 --> 01:20:41.250
leaving a stunned group of gritty owls in the lurch

01:20:41.250 --> 01:20:44.750
and committed recruits uncertain of the program's direction.

01:20:45.150 --> 01:20:47.110
Most of those recruits were still in high school,

01:20:47.410 --> 01:20:49.090
but one was already on campus

01:20:49.090 --> 01:20:51.530
and had attended his first week of classes.

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:54.040
James Casey had signed with Rice

01:20:54.040 --> 01:20:57.610
after spending four years on the baseball diamond in the low minors,

01:20:57.910 --> 01:21:00.000
chasing his dream of pitching in the majors.

01:21:00.250 --> 01:21:03.010
The challenge of stepping out of a minor league clubhouse

01:21:03.010 --> 01:21:05.930
and into the classroom of one of the top schools in the country

01:21:05.930 --> 01:21:09.250
was daunting enough, but now he had to face the added uncertainty

01:21:09.250 --> 01:21:11.350
of making his transition back to football

01:21:11.350 --> 01:21:13.710
with a staff that had not even recruited him.

01:21:14.120 --> 01:21:17.240
With only weeks to go until National Signing Day for recruits,

01:21:17.580 --> 01:21:20.200
Al's officials had to act quickly to replace Graham.

01:21:20.500 --> 01:21:23.220
After a search that included members of the stunned team,

01:21:23.630 --> 01:21:27.730
David Bailiff was introduced as the Al's 18th head football coach.

01:21:28.730 --> 01:21:30.000
The former athletic director called.

01:21:30.250 --> 01:21:34.320
and asked me to come visit with them about Rice.

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:38.210
You know, initially it jumps out in your mind

01:21:38.210 --> 01:21:41.890
that what an academic Texas treasure this is

01:21:41.890 --> 01:21:43.990
and what a prestigious university

01:21:43.990 --> 01:21:45.610
that plays Division I football.

01:21:45.610 --> 01:21:48.070
So, you know, I was very proud and humble

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:50.110
when I got the job offered

01:21:50.110 --> 01:21:53.630
and it didn't take long for me to say yes.

01:21:53.630 --> 01:21:56.610
- Bailiff's first spring proved to be an odd affair.

01:21:56.610 --> 01:21:58.720
The combination of injuries and players

01:21:58.720 --> 01:22:00.000
who had elected to not return

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:02.360
after the grueling 2006 campaign

01:22:02.360 --> 01:22:04.160
had left the new staff with less

01:22:04.160 --> 01:22:06.280
than 60 players for spring football.

01:22:06.720 --> 01:22:08.000
It was hardly an ideal

01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:10.010
introductory period. Casey,

01:22:10.230 --> 01:22:11.910
who harbored dreams of playing quarterback,

01:22:12.350 --> 01:22:14.110
was slotted at defensive end as much

01:22:14.110 --> 01:22:15.950
because of practice needs as also

01:22:15.950 --> 01:22:17.550
a true projection of his abilities.

01:22:18.480 --> 01:22:20.380
I think I got in on a Monday and that Wednesday

01:22:20.380 --> 01:22:22.650
Coach Graham just out of nowhere

01:22:22.650 --> 01:22:24.330
just says he's leaving and going to Tulsa.

01:22:24.330 --> 01:22:26.540
So we were out without a head coach

01:22:26.540 --> 01:22:28.470
and I didn't know how the scholarship process worked.

01:22:28.550 --> 01:22:30.000
I knew he gave me the scholarship so

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:34.520
I thought that my scholarship was gone now since he left so I was terrified I

01:22:34.520 --> 01:22:37.520
thought I had it you know gonna have to go somewhere else but I was shortly

01:22:37.520 --> 01:22:41.010
informed that I could stay at rice so then I was eventually during the summer

01:22:41.010 --> 01:22:44.740
they let me move over to the offensive side and and you know then of course a

01:22:44.740 --> 01:22:47.260
lot of stuff happened I ended up you know being featured in the offense my

01:22:47.260 --> 01:22:52.130
sophomore year and getting a lot of opportunities expectations for the owls

01:22:52.130 --> 01:22:56.820
in 2007 were high given the quick evaluation of the players returning from

01:22:56.820 --> 01:23:00.000
a team that had reached a bowl game the year before the reality

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:05.980
was the owls were hardly a complete team but one that had enjoyed a magical run most of it on the

01:23:05.980 --> 01:23:10.820
slender shoulders of dillard whose all-american effort had rescued the team time and time again

01:23:10.820 --> 01:23:16.000
the return of dillard and clement gave rise to great hopes but the loss of other key players

01:23:16.000 --> 01:23:21.500
including quentin smith who had quietly rushed for over 1 000 yards and left as the school's

01:23:21.500 --> 01:23:27.080
number two all-time rusher proved to be far more daunting than most wanted to admit the owls open

01:23:27.080 --> 01:23:28.250
with four straight losses

01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:30.000
and headed into a Wednesday night

01:23:30.090 --> 01:23:35.630
game with Southern Miss with an injury-depleted team. With a national audience watching on ESPN,

01:23:36.090 --> 01:23:42.030
the Owl stunned the Golden Eagles, and in the process, a legend was born. Casey had been moved

01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:46.930
from his springtime defensive end assignment to offense and had become a featured part of the Owl's

01:23:46.930 --> 01:23:52.680
short yardage attack. On this night, with the Owl shorthanded, he was called upon to use all of his

01:23:52.680 --> 01:24:00.000
skills, even those he had just developed the previous spring. In a wild affair, Casey would run, catch, block, and

01:24:00.030 --> 01:24:05.490
tackle his way to lead the Owls to a 31-29 win. He played seven different

01:24:05.490 --> 01:24:09.210
positions in the game, a fact noted nationally because of the unique

01:24:09.210 --> 01:24:13.130
Wednesday night stage the game offered. They had another couple injuries on

01:24:13.130 --> 01:24:16.220
defensive end and I was just standing around the coach and because I had the

01:24:16.220 --> 01:24:19.290
wristband I was ready to play defensive end I didn't really know if they were

01:24:19.290 --> 01:24:21.330
really gonna put me in but I was just standing there and they turned around

01:24:21.330 --> 01:24:24.410
and said get in it just just happened just like that you wouldn't know time to

01:24:24.410 --> 01:24:27.500
think it was just going to the game you know a guy like me come from minor league

01:24:27.500 --> 01:24:30.000
baseball and then going to play college football and all sudden

01:24:30.060 --> 01:24:31.280
they're talking about me nationally,

01:24:32.230 --> 01:24:34.750
I think that was really the starting point of my career,

01:24:34.910 --> 01:24:35.930
kind of my breakout game.

01:24:37.090 --> 01:24:41.020
I play in the NFL now, so that was kind of the starting point,

01:24:41.080 --> 01:24:44.280
those at Southern Miss and all the national attention I got from that game,

01:24:44.360 --> 01:24:46.550
and it was definitely a game I'll never forget.

01:24:47.590 --> 01:24:50.220
As Casey's role continued to develop,

01:24:50.580 --> 01:24:54.240
the Owls found their offensive footing over the last half of 2007,

01:24:54.240 --> 01:24:57.040
and with all the key offensive pieces returning,

01:24:57.470 --> 01:25:00.000
2008 was eagerly anticipated.

01:25:00.440 --> 01:25:05.720
The owls open the season with a pair of conference wins, including an improbable win at Memphis

01:25:05.720 --> 01:25:08.680
on an interception return in the game's final seconds.

01:25:08.680 --> 01:25:13.360
A road game at Vanderbilt was played under the uncertainty of the impact Hurricane Ike

01:25:13.360 --> 01:25:15.930
was having as it came ashore in Houston.

01:25:15.930 --> 01:25:19.660
And three weeks later, Rice stood at three and three.

01:25:19.660 --> 01:25:24.720
Again, it was Southern Miss who found themselves the opponent in a pivotal game for the owls.

01:25:24.720 --> 01:25:30.000
And Rice's 45-40 win was the start of a seven-game winning streak that saw Clement,

01:25:30.030 --> 01:25:36.130
Dillard and Casey rewrite the Rice record book and end with a happy group of teammates celebrating

01:25:36.130 --> 01:25:42.360
a Texas Bowl championship with a win over Western Michigan. Dillard smashed the NCAA career record

01:25:42.360 --> 01:25:47.740
for touchdown receptions, bettering the previous total by eight. He combined with Clement to produce

01:25:47.740 --> 01:25:53.800
the most touchdown receptions by a duo in history. And in an odd twist of fate, they switched roles

01:25:53.800 --> 01:25:59.270
late in the third quarter with Dillard throwing a TD pass to Clement as the Owls continued to pile

01:25:59.270 --> 01:26:00.000
on the points.

01:26:01.020 --> 01:26:03.120
Casey simply put together the single

01:26:03.120 --> 01:26:05.700
greatest receiving season in school history.

01:26:05.700 --> 01:26:10.570
Catching 111 passes for over 1,300 yards.

01:26:10.570 --> 01:26:12.450
Having completed everything he could

01:26:12.450 --> 01:26:15.470
on the college level, Casey set his eyes on the NFL,

01:26:15.470 --> 01:26:18.190
and his improbable story took another odd turn

01:26:18.190 --> 01:26:21.610
as the hometown Houston Texans took him in the fifth round.

01:26:21.610 --> 01:26:24.170
Assuring Owls fans more opportunities to watch him

01:26:24.170 --> 01:26:26.850
continue to develop his skills.

01:26:26.850 --> 01:26:30.000
With the Owls' success on the field, a number of area players

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:32.320
began to rethink their initial decisions

01:26:32.320 --> 01:26:34.080
to play football out of state

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:36.020
and began to transfer back to Houston.

01:26:36.400 --> 01:26:39.100
At the same time, interest in joining a program

01:26:39.100 --> 01:26:41.060
that was excelling both on the field

01:26:41.060 --> 01:26:43.480
and in the classroom continued to grow.

01:26:43.800 --> 01:26:45.540
The Owls were honored in 2010

01:26:45.540 --> 01:26:47.780
by the American Football Coaches Association

01:26:47.780 --> 01:26:50.140
for their outstanding graduation rate,

01:26:50.420 --> 01:26:52.520
and the football team continued to be ranked

01:26:52.520 --> 01:26:55.240
at the top of every NCAA standard

01:26:55.240 --> 01:26:57.220
for their academic excellence.

01:26:57.860 --> 01:27:00.000
What had begun in 1912

01:27:00.060 --> 01:27:02.560
as a mechanism to help promote and brand

01:27:02.560 --> 01:27:04.900
a fledgling institution of higher learning,

01:27:04.900 --> 01:27:08.020
continued to deliver upon that initial mission statement

01:27:08.020 --> 01:27:10.740
as both the university and football program

01:27:10.740 --> 01:27:13.250
contemplated their second centuries.

01:27:13.250 --> 01:27:15.510
That vision for the future includes plans

01:27:15.510 --> 01:27:17.790
for a revitalization of the facilities

01:27:17.790 --> 01:27:21.370
that George R. Brown had been inspired to build in 1950.

01:27:21.370 --> 01:27:25.480
At the Rice Athletics Public Forum in January of 2012,

01:27:25.480 --> 01:27:27.680
Rice President David Lebrun said

01:27:27.680 --> 01:27:30.000
the work would be a valuable asset in a

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:33.560
attracting the next generation of elves to South Maine.

01:27:33.560 --> 01:27:37.140
- I'm excited about the plan that if you haven't already seen,

01:27:37.140 --> 01:27:40.350
you'll see here tonight for football.

01:27:40.350 --> 01:27:43.930
It calls for a new football facility for our student athletes

01:27:43.930 --> 01:27:48.620
and actually not just our football athletes and football staff

01:27:48.620 --> 01:27:51.340
and an enhancement to the stadium.

01:27:51.340 --> 01:27:55.190
The new football facility will demonstrate Rice's commitment

01:27:55.190 --> 01:27:57.670
to successful athletics and intercollegiate

01:27:57.670 --> 01:28:00.000
Division 1A sports, but it depends

01:28:00.190 --> 01:28:06.480
on raising funds from rice out fans and supporters our commitment and the

01:28:06.480 --> 01:28:10.780
quality of the total experience and opportunity we offer to our student

01:28:10.780 --> 01:28:16.680
athletes will enable us to recruit the most talented student athletes who are

01:28:16.680 --> 01:28:24.620
difference makers on and off the field at rice and beyond rice from Phil Arbuckle

01:28:24.620 --> 01:28:30.000
to Jess Neely Ken Hatfield to David Bailiff Dickey Magle's historic 90

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:35.700
yard touchdown run in the Cotton Bowl to Rice's 38-14 win in the Texas Bowl.

01:28:36.320 --> 01:28:41.430
There have been exciting moments and highlights that have made up the first 100 years, but

01:28:41.430 --> 01:28:46.210
through it all, the one constant that will remain at the epicenter of Rice's football

01:28:46.210 --> 01:28:53.500
philosophy today and always will be academics first and doing things the right way, the

01:28:53.500 --> 01:28:54.420
Rice way.

01:30:19.550 --> 01:30:23.480
This is the story of an incredible, amazing football team.

01:30:23.480 --> 01:30:28.830
The 1976 Rice University Owls of the Southwest Conference.

01:30:28.830 --> 01:30:30.000
It was a team that gave the fame

01:30:30.510 --> 01:30:35.550
maximum of excitement and entertainment. But the most important contribution by

01:30:35.550 --> 01:30:40.360
this dedicated group of Rice Owl football players and coaches was the start of a

01:30:40.360 --> 01:30:45.480
program for bigger and better things to come in the future. The new Rice head

01:30:45.480 --> 01:30:50.540
coach with the apt name of Homer Rice in his first season sounded a warning to the

01:30:50.540 --> 01:30:55.670
Southwest Conference and all NCAA college football that the Owls are back.

01:30:55.670 --> 01:31:00.000
Ready to make a move to high standings and rankings

01:31:00.480 --> 01:31:05.440
crowds again and their splendid 70,000 seat rice stadium on the beautiful rice

01:31:05.440 --> 01:31:11.610
campus let's be realistic you don't jump to the top overnight but what a start

01:31:11.610 --> 01:31:17.700
this group made tommy kramer was the key man he won consensus all-american

01:31:17.700 --> 01:31:24.150
quarterback honors against fantastic odds by a brilliant play leadership and so

01:31:24.150 --> 01:31:30.000
many statistical records it would take an hour to review them all suffice to say he led the nation

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:31.940
in both passing and total offense.

01:31:32.600 --> 01:31:33.940
The senior from San Antonio

01:31:33.940 --> 01:31:35.960
had one of the great season performances

01:31:35.960 --> 01:31:38.500
of all Southwest Conference history.

01:31:40.510 --> 01:31:41.710
How about Homer Rice?

01:31:42.000 --> 01:31:44.200
He took over a grid program

01:31:44.200 --> 01:31:45.820
struggling to make a go of it.

01:31:45.820 --> 01:31:47.960
But he hadn't been a coach on the field

01:31:47.960 --> 01:31:50.040
for seven years while serving as

01:31:50.040 --> 01:31:52.280
athletic director at the University of North Carolina.

01:31:52.910 --> 01:31:53.870
Could he come back?

01:31:54.330 --> 01:31:55.560
Did he ever?

01:31:56.220 --> 01:31:58.080
The Rice Owls of Homer Rice

01:31:58.080 --> 01:32:00.000
merely produced the top

01:32:00.090 --> 01:32:05.090
in the nation same as he had done in his previous coaching season of 1968.

01:32:05.090 --> 01:32:09.170
They scored more points than any Rice University team in over 20 years

01:32:09.170 --> 01:32:13.090
and gave the fans of Rice and all college football

01:32:13.090 --> 01:32:16.090
more thrills than they had had in a long, long time.

01:32:16.090 --> 01:32:21.770
His positive attitude philosophy triggered by his new attitude technique program

01:32:21.770 --> 01:32:25.770
made an auspicious debut in 1976.

01:32:25.770 --> 01:32:30.000
In three games, the total offense for both teams combined

01:32:30.090 --> 01:32:37.520
over 1,000 yards. Kramer completed a Southwest Conference all-time record 269

01:32:37.520 --> 01:32:48.220
passes of 501 attempts for a record, 3,317 yards, and 21 touchdowns, plus running

01:32:48.220 --> 01:33:00.000
for four more touchdowns. James Sykes was just one pass away from a tie

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:07.750
for the national lead for receiving. Doug Cunningham was the national leader

01:33:07.750 --> 01:33:12.130
much of the time until a severe finger injury sidelined him in late season.

01:33:12.130 --> 01:33:20.180
David Hauser set a school record with 931 yards receiving on 52 receptions, one

01:33:20.180 --> 01:33:24.420
of the all-time highs for yardage in conference history. Kenneth Roy set an

01:33:24.420 --> 01:33:28.680
all-time selfless conference record with nine touchdown passes caught.

01:33:30.160 --> 01:33:36.500
Sykes also was the top rusher and was third in all NCAA rankings in kickoff returns.

01:33:38.180 --> 01:33:43.960
Kramer received terrific protection for a dedicated offensive line led by Danny Johnson

01:33:43.960 --> 01:33:48.140
and with help from several other fine players did a yeoman job for him.

01:33:48.140 --> 01:33:53.640
Even though the defense had problems triggered by an unusually high quota of injuries,

01:33:54.040 --> 01:33:59.340
they had some good moments and strong recruiting will help the Owls' needs in this area.

01:34:01.530 --> 01:34:06.510
But let's get on to some of the many big plays of a memorable and exciting year for the Rice

01:34:06.510 --> 01:34:12.390
Owls, a team representing the smallest school in the NCAA Division I ranks that plays the

01:34:12.390 --> 01:34:17.830
toughest schedule by far of small schools of high academic standing that compete well

01:34:17.830 --> 01:34:19.070
in the major college ranks.

01:34:19.770 --> 01:34:22.050
The opening game at Rice Stadium with Utah.

01:34:22.840 --> 01:34:30.000
Right after the kickoff, Utah tries a pass, senior cornerback Artie Segers intercepts and returns

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:44.090
it to the Utah five-yard line, the first offensive play of the season. Fires to Kenneth Roy, touchdown!

01:34:45.460 --> 01:34:50.880
And these two fine seniors, in postseason action after this epic start, play together under Coach

01:34:50.880 --> 01:34:54.140
Rice in the Blue-Gray game and the Senior Bowl game in Alabama.

01:34:54.520 --> 01:35:00.000
About 57,000 fans saw the Owls at home in Rice Stadium against the famous

01:35:00.120 --> 01:35:06.030
Daryl Royal, Texas Longhorns. Texas won, but the Owls gave the fans some thrills as

01:35:06.030 --> 01:35:15.530
Kramer completed 34 passes for 397 yards, and this one did not count. Just at the

01:35:15.530 --> 01:35:20.810
game's end, TK hit David Hauser at the sideline, and had it held up, he would

01:35:20.810 --> 01:35:24.950
have tied a conference record for yards passing in the game. It was so close.

01:35:24.950 --> 01:35:30.000
Earlier, Kramer had connected on this 14-yard touchdown shot

01:35:30.280 --> 01:35:35.610
to Doug Cunningham, a great receiver, also from San Antonio, as is Cramer,

01:35:35.610 --> 01:35:41.650
and Cunningham was a solid All-America candidate for 1977.

01:35:41.650 --> 01:35:43.870
When Texas Tech came to Rice Stadium,

01:35:43.870 --> 01:35:46.850
the Red Raiders were at a season peak of proficiency,

01:35:46.850 --> 01:35:49.810
en route to a co-championship of the conference.

01:35:49.810 --> 01:35:52.300
But the Owls had their moments in this one,

01:35:52.300 --> 01:35:56.240
such as this pair of touchdown passes by Tommy Cramer.

01:35:56.240 --> 01:35:58.710
First, a six-yard shot to David Hauser,

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:06.640
then a six-yard scoring pass to the veteran Kenneth Roy. The Owls made the

01:36:06.640 --> 01:36:11.390
long track to the Ozarks to play Arkansas in October and it was quite an

01:36:11.390 --> 01:36:17.080
event. Supposed to get all but run out of the stadium, the Rice defense had its

01:36:17.080 --> 01:36:21.500
greatest effort as they shut down the Razorbacks for a half with some

01:36:21.500 --> 01:36:27.940
defensive plays such as this interception by Don Parrish and a sack of

01:36:27.940 --> 01:36:30.000
quarterback Ron Calcagney by Dwight

01:36:30.090 --> 01:36:38.970
Moyer. Then this spectacular running play by James Sykes sparked the Owls to a

01:36:38.970 --> 01:36:44.690
first half 7 to nothing lead. The senior from Cold Spring raced 39 yards for a

01:36:44.690 --> 01:36:50.190
score. In the second half, the Owls had this dazzling 30-yard pass from Kramer to

01:36:50.190 --> 01:36:54.740
David Hauser, the sophomore flanker from Dallas, who came on at this point in

01:36:54.740 --> 01:37:00.000
midseason for a sensational stretch drive that gave him the Rice record for yards

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:07.370
receiving it was on a cold gray day at Aggieland that the rice offense had

01:37:07.370 --> 01:37:12.880
perhaps his finest hour even though a rugged Texas A&M team won the game with

01:37:12.880 --> 01:37:17.990
its own strong ground oriented offense but what a day for Kramer and the owl

01:37:17.990 --> 01:37:23.300
error core as the receiving group for TK's passes came to be known this season

01:37:23.300 --> 01:37:30.000
here is Kramer hitting Hauser for a spectacular pass and run play of 45

01:37:30.320 --> 01:37:31.280
early in the game.

01:37:32.780 --> 01:37:35.900
On this day, the Owls were to score 34 points

01:37:35.900 --> 01:37:38.860
and make 376-yard passing

01:37:38.860 --> 01:37:42.230
and 470 yards total offense

01:37:42.230 --> 01:37:46.010
against perhaps the best defensive unit in the whole nation.

01:37:47.280 --> 01:37:50.690
Now we see Kramer run one in

01:37:50.690 --> 01:37:53.100
on a quarterback sneak from the one-yard line.

01:37:54.240 --> 01:37:57.440
It had been set up by this pass to freshman Earl Cooper

01:37:57.440 --> 01:37:59.000
to the lip of the cup.

01:38:03.230 --> 01:38:07.890
The defense set up this score, we'll see in a moment, on a blocked punt by linebacker

01:38:07.890 --> 01:38:15.050
Mike Collier that gave the Owls good field position, and soon after, Kramer hit Cunningham

01:38:15.050 --> 01:38:19.370
for a 15-yard touchdown pass.

01:38:19.370 --> 01:38:25.190
With several games to go, this 12th touchdown pass of the year was a Rice season record

01:38:25.190 --> 01:38:30.000
for scoring passes in one campaign.

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:34.160
Sunningham caught this pass to set up another Kramer sneak touchdown.

01:38:34.840 --> 01:38:48.320
Recovery by Dennis Blair for the defense after a hit by Collier

01:38:48.320 --> 01:38:53.880
set up this scoring pass by Rice from Kramer to Hauser for 14 yards.

01:38:55.460 --> 01:39:00.000
Perhaps the brightest day of all for the Owls was quite

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:04.800
appropriately the homecoming game for the scrappy Southern Methodist University

01:39:04.800 --> 01:39:11.190
team. Sports Illustrated magazine had a writer on hand to detail the Owls' Tommy

01:39:11.190 --> 01:39:17.000
Kramer exploits and did he ever give them a story? It was a dramatic game that

01:39:17.000 --> 01:39:22.670
saw Rice and the Owl Air Corps rally to win in the final stages. Kramer hits Hauser

01:39:22.670 --> 01:39:30.000
for 20 yards on this play, then he comes back with this shot to sophomore Guy Booker a few

01:39:30.030 --> 01:39:40.340
and Yates to the nine-yard line that led to this Kramer quarterback keeper for a

01:39:40.340 --> 01:39:47.260
rice score next Kramer connects with sophomore Charlie Taylor of Fort Worth

01:39:47.260 --> 01:39:54.720
on this dazzling 27-yard reception to the SMU seven-yard line that sets up

01:39:54.720 --> 01:40:00.000
this scoring pass to Kenneth Roy one of his nine touchdown passes of the year

01:40:18.040 --> 01:40:25.550
in for two yards and the score. Setting up that touchdown was this Dallas

01:40:25.550 --> 01:40:30.000
Sunset product David Hauser catch of a 37-yard pass play to

01:40:30.120 --> 01:40:39.920
The Rice Owls made a late-season visit to Florida to play a strong, bowl-bound University of Florida team in Gainesville.

01:40:39.920 --> 01:40:46.500
It was sunny and warm on arrival, but a chilly and steady rain came in for game day at Florida Field.

01:40:46.500 --> 01:40:52.120
The conditions were the worst possible for the famous Owl Lercore and Tommy Kramer,

01:40:52.120 --> 01:40:56.970
but the remarkable Rice Aerial game was successful, even with the adverse conditions.

01:40:56.970 --> 01:41:00.000
Florida outscrews the West.

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:04.880
scored the Owls and played well, but Tommy Kramer and his receivers, especially David

01:41:04.880 --> 01:41:10.240
Hauser, received many accolades from the fans and newsmen in the Southeastern Conference

01:41:10.240 --> 01:41:16.990
area. Kramer graphically showed why Gator coach Doug Dickey had said at a pep rally

01:41:16.990 --> 01:41:23.990
on Friday night, "Tomorrow we face the best passer in the world, a real All-American in

01:41:23.990 --> 01:41:30.000
Tommy Kramer." Ignoring the elements, here we see Kramer connect with him.

01:41:30.060 --> 01:41:45.460
for 37 yards to the Gator 25 plays later this pass to Guy Booker was just short

01:41:45.460 --> 01:42:00.000
of the goal line Sykes took it in scoring on this run on this day how

01:42:00.090 --> 01:42:06.150
Hauser of Rice caught more passes in one game than any receiver in the nation for a game

01:42:06.150 --> 01:42:07.420
the entire season.

01:42:07.420 --> 01:42:08.510
14.

01:42:08.510 --> 01:42:12.550
Here is one to Hauser for 22 yards.

01:42:12.550 --> 01:42:17.830
And a bit later, a shot to David is for 13 yards on a Rice scoring drive that cut a Florida

01:42:17.830 --> 01:42:23.040
lead to 19 to 14 just before halftime.

01:42:23.040 --> 01:42:26.720
This Kramer to Hauser aerial gained 11 yards.

01:42:36.620 --> 01:42:42.620
he fires this one for 21 yards to his sophomore receiver who has two more years to catch passes

01:42:42.620 --> 01:42:52.650
for the rice out and then the final owl touchdown was a momentous one for it was to Kenneth Roy to

01:42:52.650 --> 01:42:58.730
set an all-time Southwest Conference record of nine touchdown passes caught in a season in the

01:42:58.730 --> 01:43:00.000
last game of the season

01:43:00.570 --> 01:43:05.770
contest in a row with no open dates. The Owls played a tough cross town and

01:43:05.770 --> 01:43:10.580
Southwest Conference rival University of Houston in Rice Stadium. In his last

01:43:10.580 --> 01:43:15.420
college game, Kramer clinched All-American honors as he completed 27

01:43:15.420 --> 01:43:21.940
passes for an amazing 409 yards and three touchdowns against the best pass

01:43:21.940 --> 01:43:28.390
defense in the conference. In a moment we'll see those three scoring plays, but

01:43:28.390 --> 01:43:30.000
first another feature of the game.

01:43:32.200 --> 01:43:39.880
all season the rice offensive line gave kramer terrific protection he was sacked just 15 times

01:43:39.880 --> 01:43:47.960
with over 500 pass attempts a fantastic record concentrate here on houston's 78 a star noted for

01:43:47.960 --> 01:43:54.530
getting to the passer and now to wrap up this 1976 rice highlight film let's see tommy kramer

01:43:54.530 --> 01:43:59.640
on those scoring tosses against a tough rival in the closing game of his owl career

01:44:00.830 --> 01:44:05.810
Homer Rice put in a tricky maneuver that helped little Joey DeQuinzio get wide

01:44:05.810 --> 01:44:11.510
open to get this perfect pass for a 62-yard scoring play. Then we pick up a

01:44:11.510 --> 01:44:17.210
23-yard pass that set up the second Rice score. This was to fellow senior Kenneth

01:44:17.210 --> 01:44:22.340
Roy, who joins TK in the postseason all-star play action in the Blue-Gray and

01:44:22.340 --> 01:44:27.960
Senior Bowl games. This pass to the Cougar 20 broke the Southwest Conference

01:44:27.960 --> 01:44:30.000
season pass yardage record for Craig.

01:44:32.780 --> 01:44:37.060
The second score itself was this 11-yard look-in shot to Hauser.

01:44:37.060 --> 01:44:41.490
The final play we showed was a real record-setter.

01:44:41.490 --> 01:44:45.710
80 yards from Kramer to fellow senior James Sykes

01:44:45.710 --> 01:44:48.690
with some nifty running by the lad from Cold Spring

01:44:48.690 --> 01:44:52.440
who missed by only one reception, tying for the national lead in receiving.

01:44:52.440 --> 01:44:55.940
This was the longest scoring pass of Kramer's career

01:44:55.940 --> 01:44:58.770
and his 21st touchdown pass of the season.

01:45:00.060 --> 01:45:02.040
So this season came to a close,

01:45:02.340 --> 01:45:06.900
but a new and bright era of rice football under Homer Rice is only beginning.

01:45:07.710 --> 01:45:09.550
Many seniors bowed out with this game.

01:45:09.890 --> 01:45:11.770
The great number nine, Tommy Kramer.

01:45:12.510 --> 01:45:16.430
Stella receivers, number 85, Roy, and number 32, Sykes.

01:45:17.290 --> 01:45:20.170
The great protector, Danny Johnson, number 74,

01:45:20.750 --> 01:45:23.710
and such others as John Standish and Steve Moore.

01:45:24.110 --> 01:45:29.890
And co-captain Mark Bacalo, number 51, at defensive linebacker.

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:33.180
Another fine linebacker, number 38, Eddie Collins.

01:45:34.080 --> 01:45:37.960
And a splendid tackle, number 97, Jeff Rose.

01:45:38.460 --> 01:45:41.040
The big guy was hampered by injuries a lot of the time,

01:45:41.380 --> 01:45:43.200
but he gave a great effort all year.

01:45:43.340 --> 01:45:47.730
But such stars as Cunningham and Hauser and many more

01:45:47.730 --> 01:45:49.910
are due back in 1977.

01:45:50.840 --> 01:45:53.700
Make your plans now to follow the Owls next fall

01:45:53.700 --> 01:45:57.330
and enjoy more of America's favorite and finest sport,

01:45:57.470 --> 01:46:00.000
college football, with a Rice Owls

01:46:00.120 --> 01:46:01.350
at Rice Stadium.
